-Contact this.

262
00:09:33,873 --> 00:09:35,810
MAN (ON RADIO): Captain
to Desert Station.

263
00:09:35,843 --> 00:09:37,778
Captain to Desert Station.

264
00:09:37,812 --> 00:09:38,745
Come in, Desert Station.

265
00:09:38,778 --> 00:09:39,247
Over.

266
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:40,447
-Hold me.

267
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:42,683
-Hey, it's droopy.

268
00:09:42,717 --> 00:09:44,385
-(GERMAN ACCENT) Rocket
working very good.

269
00:09:44,418 --> 00:09:45,853
MAN (ON RADIO): We're
1,000 miles above sea.

270
00:09:45,887 --> 00:09:47,287
-I wonder what the
boys are up to.

271
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:48,253
-Altitude, 38 miles.

272
00:09:48,287 --> 00:09:50,223
-I just discovered I
look like Robert Ryan.

273
00:09:50,257 --> 00:09:52,360
-Speed, 2000 miles an hour.

274
00:09:52,393 --> 00:09:54,895
Rocket approaching
point return back.

275
00:09:54,928 --> 00:09:57,698
-Hey, it's Dino.

276
00:09:57,732 --> 00:09:58,365
What a ding-a-ling.

277
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:09,277
Hey, Jerry, there's something
wrong with the stock footage

278
00:10:09,310 --> 00:10:10,377
simulator.

279
00:10:10,410 --> 00:10:10,912
-[INAUDIBLE]

280
00:10:14,648 --> 00:10:15,348
-Hello, Neptune.

281
00:10:15,382 --> 00:10:15,983
We are waiting.

282
00:10:16,017 --> 00:10:16,950
Come in.

283
00:10:16,983 --> 00:10:18,918
-This is Neptune,
God of the Sea.

284
00:10:18,952 --> 00:10:19,853
-You get me, Neptune?

285
00:10:19,887 --> 00:10:20,788
You get me?

286
00:10:20,822 --> 00:10:21,722
This is Desert Station.

287
00:10:21,755 --> 00:10:22,657
We are waiting.

288
00:10:22,690 --> 00:10:24,058
Anything wrong?

289
00:10:24,092 --> 00:10:25,125
-No, just everything.

290
00:10:25,158 --> 00:10:28,028
Just dump on me.

291
00:10:28,062 --> 00:10:29,030
-Oh, what's the matter?

292
00:10:33,333 --> 00:10:35,468
-Eh, Jerry, were you
messing with this?

293
00:10:35,502 --> 00:10:38,172
-Oh, that cuts it, Dean.

294
00:10:38,205 --> 00:10:39,273
Dean.

295
00:10:39,307 --> 00:10:41,342
-Desert Station, we
can't reverse the rocket.

296
00:10:41,375 --> 00:10:42,308
It's out of control.

297
00:10:42,342 --> 00:10:43,710
It won't turn back.

298
00:10:43,743 --> 00:10:45,612
The rocket isn't turning back.

299
00:10:45,645 --> 00:10:47,415
-Goodbye, Nobel.

300
00:10:47,448 --> 00:10:49,550
-It's 100 miles past us.

301
00:10:49,583 --> 00:10:52,787
120, 50, 75.

302
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:53,587
-Uh-oh.

303
00:10:53,620 --> 00:10:54,155
-200--

304
00:10:54,188 --> 00:10:55,055
-No, not 200!

305
00:10:55,088 --> 00:10:55,755
No!

306
00:10:55,788 --> 00:10:57,190
-Desert Station calling Neptune.

307
00:10:57,223 --> 00:10:59,327
MAN (ON RADIO):
Yes, Desert Station.

308
00:10:59,360 --> 00:10:59,993
-Give me that.

309
00:11:00,027 --> 00:11:01,162
-This is Dr. Michael Rostov.

310
00:11:01,195 --> 00:11:03,230
Are you sure your
reading is correct?

311
00:11:03,263 --> 00:11:04,565
Are you sure you
haven't miscalculated?

312
00:11:04,598 --> 00:11:05,733
-Oh yeah, I'm the jerk.

313
00:11:05,767 --> 00:11:07,300
It couldn't be
your crappy rocket.

314
00:11:07,333 --> 00:11:09,503
-Came in right on schedule,
on its exact course.

315
00:11:09,537 --> 00:11:11,272
-Where is it now?

316
00:11:11,305 --> 00:11:13,173
-It's getting below the horizon.

317
00:11:13,207 --> 00:11:14,242
-Hm.

318
00:11:14,275 --> 00:11:14,842
Bye.

319
00:11:14,875 --> 00:11:16,210
-Hurts, doesn't it?

320
00:11:16,243 --> 00:11:17,678
-Ouch.

321
00:11:17,712 --> 00:11:19,447
-Neptune still standing
by, Desert Station.

322
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,015
What orders?

323
00:11:21,048 --> 00:11:22,583
-Have them keep a clear channel.

324
00:11:22,617 --> 00:11:24,518
-Yes, sir.

325
00:11:24,552 --> 00:11:26,587
-Well, I better tell
June to hold supper.

326
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:27,788
-I don't like this.

327
00:11:27,822 --> 00:11:28,288
-Stand by.

328
00:11:28,322 --> 00:11:29,090
You can all clear.

329
00:11:29,123 --> 00:11:29,623
-Roger.

330
00:11:29,657 --> 00:11:31,025
Neptune standing by.

331
00:11:31,058 --> 00:11:32,025
Over.

332
00:11:32,058 --> 00:11:33,827
-Hold me.

333
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:36,397
-Dr. Briggs, traveling
three more hours

334
00:11:36,430 --> 00:11:39,467
at its present rate of speed,
same fixed course, where would

335
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:41,568
you say it would crash?

336
00:11:41,602 --> 00:11:43,203
-Well, here's us.

337
00:11:43,237 --> 00:11:45,705
-Somewhere within this radius.

338
00:11:45,738 --> 00:11:47,340
-Not good, is it?

339
00:11:47,373 --> 00:11:49,075
-No, it's not good.

340
00:11:49,108 --> 00:11:50,677
-If we can ever
recover it, we'll

341
00:11:50,710 --> 00:11:51,845
find why it didn't come back.

342
00:11:51,878 --> 00:11:53,880
-Oh, he's just
grasping at straws now.

343
00:11:53,913 --> 00:11:54,580
-Yeah.

344
00:11:54,613 --> 00:11:55,515
-You know what this test means.

345
00:11:55,548 --> 00:11:56,917
-Does this mean
we can kill stuff?

346
00:11:56,950 --> 00:11:58,652
-Right now it's a failure.

347
00:11:58,685 --> 00:12:00,453
-Oh, so we're the jerks now.

348
00:12:00,487 --> 00:12:03,323
-We've always managed to
be first in the long race

349
00:12:03,357 --> 00:12:04,592
against time.

350
00:12:04,625 --> 00:12:06,560
We proved that
with the atom bomb.

351
00:12:06,593 --> 00:12:08,995
-My head's a plaster
cast of Robert Mitchell.

352
00:12:09,028 --> 00:12:11,232
-Or if someone else should
recover it before we do--

353
00:12:11,265 --> 00:12:12,032
-Why's he looking at me?

354
00:12:12,065 --> 00:12:13,333
I didn't do anything.

355
00:12:13,367 --> 00:12:14,968
Get away!

356
00:12:15,002 --> 00:12:17,003
-It isn't the money, we spent,
it's the time out of the lives

357
00:12:17,037 --> 00:12:17,570
of men like--

358
00:12:17,603 --> 00:12:19,173
-Shame, shame, shame.

359
00:12:19,207 --> 00:12:21,375
- --Rostov and
Phillips and Briggs.

360
00:12:21,408 --> 00:12:26,613
-Wall-E, Mopey, Whitey,
the Beave, Eddie.

361
00:12:26,647 --> 00:12:28,582
-Those hours represent
a large portion

362
00:12:28,615 --> 00:12:30,017
of our nation's security.

363
00:12:30,050 --> 00:12:31,217
-And I'm talking to you, Bob.

364
00:12:31,250 --> 00:12:34,622
-If we don't get that rocket
back, we may never survive.

365
00:12:34,655 --> 00:12:38,292
You know just how
much security we lost.

366
00:12:38,325 --> 00:12:41,495
-Look, I feel bad enough, OK?

367
00:12:41,528 --> 00:12:43,597
-I'll get an OK
for a search party.

368
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:46,000
-And I don't want to
hear I don't know.

369
00:12:46,033 --> 00:12:46,733
-Jerk.

370
00:12:46,767 --> 00:12:48,368
-Who died and made him Droopy?

371
00:12:48,402 --> 00:12:49,603
-I don't know.

372
00:12:49,637 --> 00:12:50,603
- --line to the White House.

373
00:12:50,637 --> 00:12:51,305
-Oh, the White House.

374
00:12:51,338 --> 00:12:52,940
We're all impressed.

375
00:12:52,973 --> 00:12:55,775
-Adam and Eve on a
raft and a-- huh?

376
00:12:55,808 --> 00:12:57,645
-Ah, toasty fire.

377
00:12:57,678 --> 00:12:59,045
-Another [INAUDIBLE]?

378
00:12:59,078 --> 00:13:00,982
-No, thanks.

379
00:13:01,015 --> 00:13:02,282
Coffee's bad for
my sense of humor.

380
00:13:02,315 --> 00:13:04,017
-It makes me slur my words.

381
00:13:04,050 --> 00:13:05,652
-With you, I'll have to
stay on my good behavior.

382
00:13:05,685 --> 00:13:07,655
-Mrs. Roosevelt's looking fine.

383
00:13:07,688 --> 00:13:08,722
-Mm-hm.

384
00:13:08,755 --> 00:13:09,690
-Not at all.

385
00:13:09,723 --> 00:13:13,593
That's what it's there
for, Major Nolan.

386
00:13:13,627 --> 00:13:15,062
-Joe's the name, remember?

387
00:13:15,095 --> 00:13:16,363
-Tail gunner Joe.

388
00:13:16,397 --> 00:13:17,932
-Don't let the
rank frighten you.

389
00:13:17,965 --> 00:13:19,233
-Habit, I guess.

390
00:13:19,267 --> 00:13:20,400
-I guess.

391
00:13:20,433 --> 00:13:22,202
You don't seem to be running
out of inhibitions either.

392
00:13:22,235 --> 00:13:24,070
-Drink your drink, Joe.

393
00:13:24,103 --> 00:13:25,907
-Yes, Mommy.

394
00:13:25,940 --> 00:13:27,007
-Marla, I--

395
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:29,343
-Tell me more about your
being a flight instructor.

396
00:13:29,377 --> 00:13:31,978
The boys you trained to fly--
what are they like today?

397
00:13:32,012 --> 00:13:32,780
-Oh, they're dead.

398
00:13:32,813 --> 00:13:33,780
[CLEARS THROAT] Sorry.

399
00:13:37,150 --> 00:13:38,218
-Well, they're--

400
00:13:38,252 --> 00:13:39,553
-No more hot-rod jockey pod.

401
00:13:39,587 --> 00:13:41,222
There's no more flying by
the seat of their pants.

402
00:13:41,255 --> 00:13:42,388
These boys think.

403
00:13:42,422 --> 00:13:44,725
The day of the prop-on
planes is over.

404
00:13:44,758 --> 00:13:46,527
-These boys flying today
have to know their engineers.

405
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:48,062
-Just keep talking, dickweed.

406
00:13:48,095 --> 00:13:50,730
-They're after the same kind
of brainpower that MIT is,

407
00:13:50,763 --> 00:13:52,700
or Carnegie Tech, or--

408
00:13:52,733 --> 00:13:53,567
-Hey.

409
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:55,835
-Hey, wait a minute.

410
00:13:55,868 --> 00:13:58,972
You had me doing this
same show the other night.

411
00:13:59,005 --> 00:14:00,673
-I just wanted to see if
you had any new material.

412
00:14:00,707 --> 00:14:01,975
-It was-- let me see.

413
00:14:02,008 --> 00:14:04,445
You were taking me up to
the door to say goodnight.

414
00:14:04,478 --> 00:14:06,047
-You tried to use me as a key.

415
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:07,147
-Oh, I remember.

416
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:08,615
You wanted to come
inside and show me

417
00:14:08,648 --> 00:14:10,883
how you simulated
landings at 10,000.

418
00:14:10,917 --> 00:14:12,687
-Oh, I was drunk, wasn't I?

419
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:13,320
-Oh, sure.

420
00:14:13,353 --> 00:14:14,153
What's the matter with me?

421
00:14:14,187 --> 00:14:15,688
That was Tuesday
night I saw you.

422
00:14:15,722 --> 00:14:16,623
-Mm, no.

423
00:14:16,657 --> 00:14:18,225
-Wednesday.

424
00:14:18,258 --> 00:14:21,328
-Oh, then your name's
not Betty, is it?

425
00:14:21,362 --> 00:14:22,062
-Yeah, sure.

426
00:14:22,095 --> 00:14:22,730
Of course.

427
00:14:22,763 --> 00:14:23,463
Wednesday.

428
00:14:23,497 --> 00:14:24,130
-Doh.

429
00:14:24,163 --> 00:14:25,032
-Crash landings?

430
00:14:25,065 --> 00:14:27,802
-Nope.

431
00:14:27,835 --> 00:14:28,735
-Parachute jumps?

432
00:14:28,768 --> 00:14:30,103
-Nope.

433
00:14:30,137 --> 00:14:30,737
-Clown suits?

434
00:14:30,770 --> 00:14:31,238
-Yeah.

435
00:14:31,272 --> 00:14:32,505
-Take-offs?

436
00:14:32,538 --> 00:14:34,908
-I was tired that night, Joe.

437
00:14:34,942 --> 00:14:36,843
You left me at the front door.

438
00:14:36,877 --> 00:14:37,778
-Aw.

439
00:14:37,812 --> 00:14:38,812
-Oh, please don't get up.

440
00:14:38,845 --> 00:14:39,847
I'll change the record.

441
00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:40,880
-Oh, jeez.
I really dropped the ball.

442
00:14:40,913 --> 00:14:41,815
Gotta stay in control.

443
00:14:41,848 --> 00:14:42,717
I'm still a champ.

444
00:14:42,750 --> 00:14:44,752
I can do it.

445
00:14:44,785 --> 00:14:50,057
-Hm, hey look. "The Dead," "Mel
Torme," and here's one of mine.

446
00:14:50,090 --> 00:14:52,092
Huh?

447
00:14:52,125 --> 00:14:57,665
[MUSIC PLAYING]

448
00:15:01,035 --> 00:15:01,802
-He is neat.

449
00:15:01,835 --> 00:15:04,003
Really nice caboose.

450
00:15:04,037 --> 00:15:05,372
-Mm.

451
00:15:05,405 --> 00:15:07,073
Oh, Marcel Marceau's album.

452
00:15:07,107 --> 00:15:08,175
-Dinner was magnificent.

453
00:15:08,208 --> 00:15:10,743
-Oh, we did eat
dinner, didn't we?

454
00:15:10,777 --> 00:15:12,278
-Let's give the music
a break, shall we?

455
00:15:12,312 --> 00:15:13,580
-Oh, would you give us a break?

456
00:15:13,613 --> 00:15:14,448
-Oh, yeah.

457
00:15:14,482 --> 00:15:15,448
- --worry about my figure.

458
00:15:15,482 --> 00:15:16,783
A woman appreciates
such thoughtfulness.

459
00:15:16,817 --> 00:15:19,787
-I'll soon have you down
to fighting weight, honey.

460
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:21,688
-What a shame.

461
00:15:21,722 --> 00:15:22,422
-I lead.

462
00:15:22,455 --> 00:15:23,857
Give to Cesar what is Cesar's.

463
00:15:23,890 --> 00:15:25,858
-Fly a plane as
well as you dance?

464
00:15:25,892 --> 00:15:27,795
-I manage.

465
00:15:27,828 --> 00:15:29,330
Those simulated landings--

466
00:15:29,363 --> 00:15:33,867
[SLURPING]

467
00:15:33,900 --> 00:15:35,635
-Oh, you taste like Bud Abbott.

468
00:15:35,668 --> 00:15:37,070
-Get the clown suit.

469
00:15:37,103 --> 00:15:38,405
-Well, it certainly
took you a long time

470
00:15:38,438 --> 00:15:39,740
getting around to that, Major.

471
00:15:39,773 --> 00:15:40,973
-It will never happen again.

472
00:15:41,007 --> 00:15:42,275
[DOOR BUZZING]

473
00:15:42,308 --> 00:15:43,910
-Oh, it's like an
Operation game.

474
00:15:43,943 --> 00:15:45,378
Touche her there and she buzzes.

475
00:15:45,412 --> 00:15:46,847
-Remove tongue from tonsils.

476
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:47,513
-There's the door.

477
00:15:47,547 --> 00:15:49,650
-I don't hear anything.

478
00:15:49,683 --> 00:15:51,252
-Maybe you better answer it.

479
00:15:51,285 --> 00:15:52,653
-It's my electric truss.

480
00:15:55,488 --> 00:15:59,927
-Great to see she's
got a nice back, ay?

481
00:15:59,960 --> 00:16:00,627
-Excuse me, Major.

482
00:16:00,660 --> 00:16:01,828
Are you wearing your jimmy hat?

483
00:16:01,862 --> 00:16:02,428
-Pardon me, ma'am.

484
00:16:02,462 --> 00:16:03,363
But is Major Nolan here?

485
00:16:03,397 --> 00:16:05,098
-Oh, is that who I am?

486
00:16:05,132 --> 00:16:06,100
-Hi.

487
00:16:06,133 --> 00:16:07,233
-What is it, Sergeant?

488
00:16:07,267 --> 00:16:08,168
-My neck, sir.

489
00:16:08,202 --> 00:16:09,403
It's fused to my spine.

490
00:16:09,437 --> 00:16:11,638
Ow, ow, ow.

491
00:16:11,672 --> 00:16:14,608
-Orders for you to report
back to the base at once, sir.

492
00:16:14,642 --> 00:16:16,643
They'll have a plane warming
up for you at the flight line.

493
00:16:16,677 --> 00:16:18,112
-I was just warming up myself.

494
00:16:18,145 --> 00:16:18,678
-At once?

495
00:16:18,712 --> 00:16:20,580
-At once, sir.

496
00:16:20,613 --> 00:16:21,882
-All right, Sergeant.

497
00:16:21,915 --> 00:16:23,317
-Oh, well.

498
00:16:23,350 --> 00:16:25,085
-Come back soon, Joe.

499
00:16:25,118 --> 00:16:27,087
-And, uh, bring your jimmy hat.

500
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:28,322
-I'd like to finish our--

501
00:16:28,355 --> 00:16:29,657
[THROATS CLEARING]

502
00:16:29,690 --> 00:16:31,692
- --discussion about
simulated landings.

503
00:16:31,725 --> 00:16:34,762
[WHISTLING]

504
00:16:34,795 --> 00:16:36,430
-What time is it, Sergeant?

505
00:16:36,463 --> 00:16:37,163
-2:30, sir.

506
00:16:37,197 --> 00:16:38,163
-Hold back on the stick.

507
00:16:38,197 --> 00:16:39,967
-Well, it's a whole lot
better than near 3:00.

508
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,168
-Mm, mwah!

509
00:16:41,202 --> 00:16:44,505
-She's not bad, sir, if you
don't mind me saying it.

510
00:16:44,538 --> 00:16:46,507
-I don't mean to sound personal,
Sergeant, but how did you--

511
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:47,842
-Just following orders, Major.

512
00:16:47,875 --> 00:16:50,810
If I hadn't have come across
this at your quarters, I, uh,

513
00:16:50,843 --> 00:16:52,712
might not have found you
for another 30 minutes.

514
00:16:52,745 --> 00:16:54,882
-Uh, he's pretty old for
a sergeant, isn't he?

515
00:16:54,915 --> 00:16:56,048
-He must have
punched an officer.

516
00:16:56,082 --> 00:16:57,752
-Very careless of you, sir.

517
00:16:57,785 --> 00:17:00,187
-Give me that.

518
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:01,187
-These are all guys!

519
00:17:01,220 --> 00:17:03,523
-If you don't mind my
saying so, sir, you're

520
00:17:03,557 --> 00:17:05,025
an awfully tough man to follow.

521
00:17:05,058 --> 00:17:05,692
-She's hot.

522
00:17:05,725 --> 00:17:08,362
-Why I oughta-- knucklehead.

523
00:17:08,395 --> 00:17:09,028
-I'll be right back.

524
00:17:09,062 --> 00:17:12,032
[MONKEY NOISES]

525
00:17:12,065 --> 00:17:13,233
-What was that guy's name again?

526
00:17:13,267 --> 00:17:14,233
I remember.

527
00:17:14,267 --> 00:17:15,335
-Uh, the guy who was on
"The Danny Thomas Show."

528
00:17:15,368 --> 00:17:17,772
-Oh, that's his name.

529
00:17:17,805 --> 00:17:18,872
-I don't want any
more complaints

530
00:17:18,905 --> 00:17:19,940
out of you, sweetheart.

531
00:17:19,973 --> 00:17:20,973
Willie gave you all new parts.

532
00:17:21,007 --> 00:17:21,942
Now use 'em.

533
00:17:21,975 --> 00:17:23,577
And you, my blood you got.

534
00:17:23,610 --> 00:17:24,912
Half the night I spent over you.

535
00:17:24,945 --> 00:17:25,878
What happens?

536
00:17:25,912 --> 00:17:27,247
Wrong size.

537
00:17:27,280 --> 00:17:29,048
Now you got a new one that fits.

538
00:17:29,082 --> 00:17:29,717
Use it.

539
00:17:29,750 --> 00:17:31,452
Be relaxed and satisfied.

540
00:17:31,485 --> 00:17:32,052
-Sergeant Tatlow.

541
00:17:32,085 --> 00:17:32,752
-Hey, it does talk.

542
00:17:32,785 --> 00:17:33,787
-The CO wants you.

543
00:17:33,820 --> 00:17:34,887
-Oh, it's him.

544
00:17:34,920 --> 00:17:36,323
-What, and leave us sitting
here practically naked?

545
00:17:36,357 --> 00:17:37,657
-Well, put your pants
on and get going.

546
00:17:37,690 --> 00:17:39,660
- --hot and cold baths
in a straight jacket,

547
00:17:39,693 --> 00:17:40,560
that's your business.

548
00:17:40,593 --> 00:17:42,528
Mine is to take you to the CO.

549
00:17:42,562 --> 00:17:43,863
You coming?

550
00:17:43,897 --> 00:17:45,632
-Orders, heh.

551
00:17:45,665 --> 00:17:46,767
It's all they know.

552
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:48,302
They get 'em, they give 'em.

553
00:17:48,335 --> 00:17:49,803
You get 'em, you're stuck.

554
00:17:49,837 --> 00:17:51,372
And does your draft board care?

555
00:17:51,405 --> 00:17:52,305
Eh.

556
00:17:52,338 --> 00:17:53,273
-Eh.
-Come on.

557
00:17:53,307 --> 00:17:54,207
-Eh.
-Eh.

558
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:54,742
-Eh.

559
00:17:58,545 --> 00:17:59,547
-Mwah.

560
00:17:59,580 --> 00:18:01,315
I love this old suit.

561
00:18:01,348 --> 00:18:02,448
Ah.

562
00:18:02,482 --> 00:18:04,250
-Everybody talks to inanimate
objects in this movie.

563
00:18:04,283 --> 00:18:04,950
Pretty strange.

564
00:18:04,983 --> 00:18:06,187
-Now, don't misunderstand me.

565
00:18:06,220 --> 00:18:07,720
I don't want to make
you feel terrible.

566
00:18:07,753 --> 00:18:10,123
But I always said I've
never die in a plane.

567
00:18:10,157 --> 00:18:11,592
-But I've changed my mind.

568
00:18:11,625 --> 00:18:12,827
-Let me see.

569
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:14,427
Aw.

570
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:16,197
-Mother.

571
00:18:16,230 --> 00:18:17,932
-She got married last month.

572
00:18:17,965 --> 00:18:21,000
-That makes me legitimate now.

573
00:18:21,033 --> 00:18:22,502
-Ah, my brother Russell.

574
00:18:22,535 --> 00:18:25,605
He made the pep squad.

575
00:18:25,638 --> 00:18:27,073
-I'll keep them flying, Jackie.

576
00:18:27,107 --> 00:18:28,040
Follow my leave.

577
00:18:28,073 --> 00:18:29,242
I'll tell you all about
it when I get back.

578
00:18:29,275 --> 00:18:31,010
-[LAUGHS] Here we go.

579
00:18:31,043 --> 00:18:31,945
Huh?

580
00:18:31,978 --> 00:18:32,478
Oh.

581
00:18:32,512 --> 00:18:33,880
-Lieutenant Wilson?

582
00:18:33,913 --> 00:18:34,380
-Where?

583
00:18:34,413 --> 00:18:36,750
-One L or two?

584
00:18:36,783 --> 00:18:38,652
-Uh, five!

585
00:18:38,685 --> 00:18:39,520
-Lieutenant Daniel Wilson.

586
00:18:39,553 --> 00:18:40,887
-Got your library card, sir.

587
00:18:40,920 --> 00:18:43,490
-CO wants you right away.

588
00:18:43,523 --> 00:18:44,992
-Uh-oh.

589
00:18:45,025 --> 00:18:47,593
-Dame said she voted last year.

590
00:18:47,627 --> 00:18:49,428
-What?

591
00:18:49,462 --> 00:18:50,563
-Maybe she lied.

592
00:18:50,597 --> 00:18:51,432
I can't understand it.

593
00:18:51,465 --> 00:18:52,132
-Woosh.

594
00:18:52,165 --> 00:18:53,533
I think that's over our heads.

595
00:18:53,567 --> 00:18:54,668
-As I was telling
that other guy,

596
00:18:54,702 --> 00:18:56,337
I never wanted to
die in a plane.

597
00:18:56,370 --> 00:18:58,105
But now I'm very
excited about it.

598
00:19:04,845 --> 00:19:06,380
-All right, give me
another Rum and Diet.

599
00:19:06,413 --> 00:19:07,915
Then we auger it in, Phil.

600
00:19:10,450 --> 00:19:12,252
-Dad, Dad, can we stop?

601
00:19:12,285 --> 00:19:13,520
I gotta go to the bathroom.

602
00:19:13,553 --> 00:19:14,520
-Hey, come on.
-We're gonna get there.

603
00:19:14,553 --> 00:19:15,155
Are we there yet?

604
00:19:15,188 --> 00:19:16,288
Can we stop?

605
00:19:16,322 --> 00:19:18,158
-I lost out on a little
contentment and peace myself.

606
00:19:18,192 --> 00:19:18,692
-What?

607
00:19:18,725 --> 00:19:20,127
-So why pick on me?

608
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:22,062
-Because I missed your
ugly face, Danny boy.

609
00:19:22,095 --> 00:19:22,963
I got lonesome.

610
00:19:22,997 --> 00:19:24,197
-Come on.

611
00:19:24,230 --> 00:19:25,465
-Because you were so pleasant
and uncomplaining those two

612
00:19:25,498 --> 00:19:28,135
delightful years we spent living
in that romantic South Pacific

613
00:19:28,168 --> 00:19:29,135
jungle together.

614
00:19:29,168 --> 00:19:30,737
-Jeez, everything's a
speech with these guys.

615
00:19:30,770 --> 00:19:32,172
-We got to crash
on an island that's

616
00:19:32,205 --> 00:19:33,940
loaded with
guerrilla resistance.

617
00:19:33,973 --> 00:19:36,208
-You made it sound so
good, I'm taking us in.

618
00:19:36,242 --> 00:19:37,375
And

619
00:19:37,408 --> 00:19:38,712
-Then in two seconds, you're
a South Pacific commando.

620
00:19:38,745 --> 00:19:39,913
What a picnic.

621
00:19:39,947 --> 00:19:40,947
-You got liberated.

622
00:19:40,980 --> 00:19:42,782
-Yeah, I got liberated.

623
00:19:42,815 --> 00:19:43,417
-So did she.

624
00:19:43,450 --> 00:19:43,983
Boom.

625
00:19:44,017 --> 00:19:45,785
-Look where it got me.

626
00:19:45,818 --> 00:19:47,887
-Liberation, it's
a wonderful thing.

627
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:48,453
-Hm?

628
00:19:48,487 --> 00:19:49,353
-There's strength to it.

629
00:19:49,387 --> 00:19:50,690
-Oh, it must be a
Northwestern flight.

630
00:19:50,723 --> 00:19:51,592
Huh?

631
00:19:51,625 --> 00:19:52,258
-Hm?

632
00:19:52,292 --> 00:19:52,993
-Uh, yeah.

633
00:19:53,027 --> 00:19:54,393
-How about some coffee?

634
00:19:54,427 --> 00:19:55,360
-No thanks.

635
00:19:55,393 --> 00:19:56,330
I already had mine.
[LAUGHS] I love that.

636
00:19:56,363 --> 00:19:57,563
-If it's not one
thing, it's another.

637
00:19:57,597 --> 00:20:00,833
-Coming right up, sir.

638
00:20:00,867 --> 00:20:02,602
-Eh, I gotta-- oh, ah.

639
00:20:02,635 --> 00:20:03,637
Forget that rest stop.

640
00:20:03,670 --> 00:20:05,038
I'm fine now.

641
00:20:05,072 --> 00:20:08,508
-Meanwhile over Ames, Iowa--

642
00:20:08,542 --> 00:20:11,278
-That guys' kind of a
chunky steward, isn't he?

643
00:20:11,312 --> 00:20:12,478
-Mm.

644
00:20:12,512 --> 00:20:13,413
-Yeah, pardon me, Doc.

645
00:20:13,447 --> 00:20:15,082
I brought some coffee.

646
00:20:15,115 --> 00:20:18,985
-Oh, uh, I-- I wasn't
doing anything. [LAUGHS]

647
00:20:19,018 --> 00:20:19,787
-Smells good.

648
00:20:19,820 --> 00:20:20,787
-It's just me, oof.

649
00:20:20,820 --> 00:20:21,720
-Let me tell you, Doc.

650
00:20:21,753 --> 00:20:22,855
Everything about this
doll smells good.

651
00:20:22,888 --> 00:20:24,290
Just look at the fuselage.

652
00:20:24,323 --> 00:20:25,525
Really stacked, eh?

653
00:20:25,558 --> 00:20:27,627
-Oh, you've been alone too
long, haven't you, Sergeant?

654
00:20:27,660 --> 00:20:28,528
-Listen to her sing.

655
00:20:28,562 --> 00:20:29,562
I tell you, she's a dream, Doc.

656
00:20:29,595 --> 00:20:30,597
My baby.

657
00:20:30,630 --> 00:20:31,630
-If she can only cook.

658
00:20:31,663 --> 00:20:34,267
-Hey, you're talking
about the woman he loves.

659
00:20:34,300 --> 00:20:37,037
-Aw, Doc, hey, I
can see you haven't

660
00:20:37,070 --> 00:20:38,438
had much experience with things.

661
00:20:38,472 --> 00:20:41,542
You know, the first
thing you got to know

662
00:20:41,575 --> 00:20:43,643
is never-- never
spoil their makeup.

663
00:20:43,677 --> 00:20:45,712
-Ah, uh-huh. [LAUGH]
You're really

664
00:20:45,745 --> 00:20:48,048
bucking for that
Section Eight, pal.

665
00:20:48,082 --> 00:20:49,515
-I'm the wrong guy to ask, Doc.

666
00:20:49,548 --> 00:20:50,783
You know, looking
out from high places

667
00:20:50,817 --> 00:20:53,887
makes little black spots do
a ballet in front of my eyes.

668
00:20:53,920 --> 00:20:56,890
-(RUSSIAN ACCENT) I can
prove these guys don't exist.

669
00:20:56,923 --> 00:20:58,192
-Thank you, Mick.

670
00:20:58,225 --> 00:20:59,960
-Remind me to kill you later.

671
00:20:59,993 --> 00:21:03,097
-Uh, got some
coffee for you, Doc.

672
00:21:03,130 --> 00:21:03,663
-Coffee?

673
00:21:03,697 --> 00:21:04,898
-Yeah, it's a beverage.

674
00:21:04,932 --> 00:21:06,165
-Oh, yes, thanks.

675
00:21:06,198 --> 00:21:08,302
Oh, it's just what I
need about this time.

676
00:21:08,335 --> 00:21:09,903
-Oh, definitely.

677
00:21:09,937 --> 00:21:12,438
-Uh, and suicide
has a U in it, sir.

678
00:21:12,472 --> 00:21:14,842
-You get out of here.

679
00:21:14,875 --> 00:21:17,277
Doh.

680
00:21:17,310 --> 00:21:17,777
-Doo-doo-do.

681
00:21:17,810 --> 00:21:19,245
Huh?

682
00:21:19,278 --> 00:21:23,617
[MONKEY NOISES]

683
00:21:23,650 --> 00:21:26,520
-Uh, genocide has
a C in it, sir.

684
00:21:26,553 --> 00:21:29,823
-(GERMAN ACCENT) Would
you get out of here?

685
00:21:29,857 --> 00:21:30,757
-Oh.

686
00:21:30,790 --> 00:21:31,823
Heh.

687
00:21:31,857 --> 00:21:33,460
I don't know about you,
Doc, but these long trips

688
00:21:33,493 --> 00:21:34,660
always get me a little sleepy.

689
00:21:34,693 --> 00:21:36,897
Figured a little coffee might
keep us all awake, Yeah.

690
00:21:36,930 --> 00:21:37,930
-(GERMAN ACCENT) I don't sleep.

691
00:21:37,963 --> 00:21:41,702
Get away from me,
little monkey boy.

692
00:21:41,735 --> 00:21:43,237
-Man, don't say hi.

693
00:21:43,270 --> 00:21:44,170
Jeepers.

694
00:21:44,203 --> 00:21:45,405
-(GERMAN ACCENT)
We'll hollow you out

695
00:21:45,438 --> 00:21:49,042
and use you as a sleeping bag?

696
00:21:49,075 --> 00:21:51,177
-You ever fly one
of these before?

697
00:21:51,210 --> 00:21:51,945
-Nope.

698
00:21:51,978 --> 00:21:53,047
-Neither have I.

699
00:21:53,080 --> 00:21:53,880
-OK.

700
00:21:53,913 --> 00:21:55,515
-OK, let's see here.

701
00:21:55,548 --> 00:21:58,017
Who had the Manhattan, and
who had 15 Rum and Diet Cokes?

702
00:21:58,050 --> 00:22:00,453
Good thing we
stocked up at Fargo.

703
00:22:00,487 --> 00:22:01,453
-Oh, what characters.

704
00:22:01,487 --> 00:22:03,323
You're as bad as plying
a bunch of congressmen

705
00:22:03,357 --> 00:22:05,058
to visit the war front.

706
00:22:05,092 --> 00:22:07,493
-Really, how you talk.

707
00:22:07,527 --> 00:22:08,795
This is a posh assignment.

708
00:22:08,828 --> 00:22:10,763
-What a fem.

709
00:22:10,797 --> 00:22:13,233
-We're hauling VIPs,
distinguished scientists.

710
00:22:13,267 --> 00:22:14,433
-Ha, ha, hoo.

711
00:22:14,467 --> 00:22:16,803
-Sergeant, you should have
brewed them some liquid

712
00:22:16,837 --> 00:22:19,205
hydrogen served
with smashed atoms--

713
00:22:19,238 --> 00:22:20,440
-Somebody's hurting.

714
00:22:20,473 --> 00:22:21,040
-Mm-hm.

715
00:22:21,073 --> 00:22:21,607
-Yeah.

716
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:22,640
Yeah, I'll serve 'em.

717
00:22:22,675 --> 00:22:24,677
One joke was taking
pictures through a window.

718
00:22:24,710 --> 00:22:26,580
Another one looks like he's
writing a letter to Einstein.

719
00:22:26,613 --> 00:22:28,382
That Rostov guy is
playing games with a map.

720
00:22:28,415 --> 00:22:29,015
But what do we do?

721
00:22:29,048 --> 00:22:30,350
Go chasing after a rocket.

722
00:22:30,383 --> 00:22:31,985
-That's a naughty
word, Sergeant.

723
00:22:32,018 --> 00:22:33,052
Top secret.

724
00:22:33,085 --> 00:22:34,387
Hey, take over.

725
00:22:34,420 --> 00:22:34,953
-Me?

726
00:22:34,987 --> 00:22:35,887
I'm going a Sergeant.

727
00:22:35,920 --> 00:22:37,957
-I'm going to play
Boy Scout back there,

728
00:22:37,990 --> 00:22:39,592
let you two
aggravate each other.

729
00:22:39,625 --> 00:22:40,460
-Let's get out of here.

730
00:22:40,493 --> 00:22:41,327
-Ah.

731
00:22:41,360 --> 00:22:42,228
-Eh.

732
00:22:42,262 --> 00:22:43,130
Pick me.

733
00:22:43,163 --> 00:22:45,998
[GRUMBLING]

734
00:22:46,032 --> 00:22:47,600
-Hey, did you ever fly
one of these things?

735
00:22:47,633 --> 00:22:49,468
-Ulcers, he says.

736
00:22:49,502 --> 00:22:51,805
My ulcers never had us a good
[INAUDIBLE] with me this trip.

737
00:22:59,012 --> 00:23:01,247
-Turns off the hose,
climbs down to the ladder,

738
00:23:01,280 --> 00:23:03,983
turns to the clown, and says,
what, and quit show business?

739
00:23:04,017 --> 00:23:05,685
[LAUGHS]

740
00:23:05,718 --> 00:23:07,353
-Tom, I'd really love
to laugh at that.

741
00:23:07,387 --> 00:23:08,722
But I'm the one
who told it to you.

742
00:23:08,755 --> 00:23:10,423
Essentially, I programmed
it into your memory.

743
00:23:10,457 --> 00:23:10,923
Remember?

744
00:23:10,957 --> 00:23:11,590
-Oh, yeah.

745
00:23:11,623 --> 00:23:12,458
-Oh, here's one you'll like.

746
00:23:12,492 --> 00:23:13,260
You'll want to get this.

747
00:23:13,293 --> 00:23:14,160
You'll like this.

748
00:23:14,193 --> 00:23:15,193
OK.

749
00:23:15,227 --> 00:23:17,163
There's these four monks,
an albino squirrel,

750
00:23:17,197 --> 00:23:18,265
and a shoe factory.

751
00:23:18,298 --> 00:23:19,198
OK.

752
00:23:19,232 --> 00:23:20,500
-Wait, there's a ship
coming into range.

753
00:23:20,533 --> 00:23:22,135
-OK, you can tell you
joke when I'm done.

754
00:23:22,168 --> 00:23:22,635
-Uh, no.

755
00:23:22,668 --> 00:23:23,737
No, it's no joke.

756
00:23:23,770 --> 00:23:26,072
-But no-- or your anecdote,
or whatever you got.

757
00:23:26,105 --> 00:23:28,142
-But anyway there's
these four monks.

758
00:23:28,175 --> 00:23:29,810
And they work on a
construction crew.

759
00:23:29,843 --> 00:23:30,910
This is neat.

760
00:23:30,943 --> 00:23:32,945
They work on a construction
crew at the monastery.

761
00:23:32,978 --> 00:23:34,647
OK And they just
get done working--

762
00:23:34,680 --> 00:23:35,513
-Joel, Joel, Joel.

763
00:23:35,547 --> 00:23:36,382
- on some of the
stations of the cross.

764
00:23:36,415 --> 00:23:37,182
-Joel, Joel, Joel.

765
00:23:37,215 --> 00:23:37,983
-And they see this
albino squirrel,

766
00:23:38,017 --> 00:23:39,518
who carries it really well.

767
00:23:39,552 --> 00:23:40,020
And--

768
00:23:40,053 --> 00:23:40,687
-Joel, Joel.

769
00:23:40,720 --> 00:23:41,722
-Oh, would you cut that out?

770
00:23:41,755 --> 00:23:42,922
-Well, hey.

771
00:23:42,955 --> 00:23:43,955
Jeez, what a grouch.

772
00:23:43,988 --> 00:23:45,158
It's the only way I
could get your attention.

773
00:23:45,192 --> 00:23:46,993
-Hey, there's a ship
coming into range.

774
00:23:47,027 --> 00:23:47,560
-[SIGH]

775
00:23:47,593 --> 00:23:48,662
-Willikers, he's right.

776
00:23:48,695 --> 00:23:51,598
Cambot, give me Rocket
Number Nine, pronto.

777
00:23:51,632 --> 00:23:58,505
[DOORBELL]

778
00:23:58,538 --> 00:23:59,907
-Hello, boys.

779
00:23:59,940 --> 00:24:00,907
-Hugh Beaumont.

780
00:24:00,940 --> 00:24:01,440
-Oh, come on.

781
00:24:01,473 --> 00:24:02,808
Call me dad.

782
00:24:02,842 --> 00:24:03,443
-Gee, sir.

783
00:24:03,477 --> 00:24:04,043
I mean, Dad.

784
00:24:04,077 --> 00:24:05,578
What are you doing here?

785
00:24:05,612 --> 00:24:06,645
-Well, you see boys.

786
00:24:06,678 --> 00:24:09,448
I'm one of the Four
Horsemen of the Apocalypse.

787
00:24:09,482 --> 00:24:13,152
And, uh, I come bearing a
message of unholy death.

788
00:24:13,185 --> 00:24:13,953
-Huh?

789
00:24:13,987 --> 00:24:14,920
-That's right.

790
00:24:14,953 --> 00:24:16,255
I'm really going to
give you the business.

791
00:24:16,288 --> 00:24:18,525
Destroy you, your world,
and all that you know.

792
00:24:18,558 --> 00:24:21,595
But first, a stern talking to.

793
00:24:21,628 --> 00:24:24,797
You know, you boys shouldn't
be so hard on that movie.

794
00:24:24,830 --> 00:24:26,933
A lot of people worked
quite hard to bring

795
00:24:26,967 --> 00:24:28,200
that movie to you.

796
00:24:28,233 --> 00:24:30,603
-Gee, I guess we shouldn't be
so quick to judge others then,

797
00:24:30,637 --> 00:24:31,137
huh?

798
00:24:31,170 --> 00:24:31,905
-That's right, Crow.

799
00:24:31,938 --> 00:24:33,005
-(WHISPERING) He bought it.

800
00:24:33,038 --> 00:24:34,507
What a jerk.

801
00:24:34,540 --> 00:24:36,342
-Well, I'm glad we learned
a little lesson then.

802
00:24:36,375 --> 00:24:38,912
Well, it's time to die.

803
00:24:38,945 --> 00:24:39,612
-Gee, sir.

804
00:24:39,645 --> 00:24:41,113
I mean, Dad.

805
00:24:41,147 --> 00:24:43,015
Why must you kill
all life and matter?

806
00:24:43,048 --> 00:24:44,952
-Yeah, can't you give
us another chance?

807
00:24:44,985 --> 00:24:46,152
-Oh, please, Dad.

808
00:24:46,185 --> 00:24:49,890
Don't kill us take our souls
to the afterworld and stuff.

809
00:24:49,923 --> 00:24:53,427
-Boys, I-- I think I
owe you an apology.

810
00:24:53,460 --> 00:24:55,628
I guess your mother
and I got so wrapped up

811
00:24:55,662 --> 00:24:57,230
in this apocalypse
thing I didn't

812
00:24:57,263 --> 00:24:58,765
have time to think how you felt.

813
00:24:58,798 --> 00:25:00,667
-Well, then you're not
going to destroy us?

814
00:25:00,700 --> 00:25:02,868
-Oh, I don't see how I could.

815
00:25:02,902 --> 00:25:04,170
Well, I've got to get going.

816
00:25:04,203 --> 00:25:06,205
I've got to catch up to
Fred MacMurray and the boys.

817
00:25:06,238 --> 00:25:08,007
They're about to destroy Earth.

818
00:25:08,040 --> 00:25:10,410
Drink your milk.

819
00:25:10,443 --> 00:25:12,512
-What a sweet guy.

820
00:25:12,545 --> 00:25:13,480
-Yeah.

821
00:25:13,513 --> 00:25:19,318
[MUSIC PLAYING]

822
00:25:22,188 --> 00:25:25,192
-Once so, we invade Poland.

823
00:25:25,225 --> 00:25:27,227
-Hey, I can see my
house from here.

824
00:25:27,260 --> 00:25:28,093
-Yeah.

825
00:25:28,127 --> 00:25:29,728
You ever fly one
of these things?

826
00:25:29,762 --> 00:25:30,230
-No.

827
00:25:30,263 --> 00:25:31,097
-Me neither.

828
00:25:31,130 --> 00:25:32,832
-Looks like this is it.

829
00:25:32,865 --> 00:25:34,935
-Oh, Stucky's.

830
00:25:34,968 --> 00:25:37,537
-We're now approaching
rocket's point of departure

831
00:25:37,570 --> 00:25:40,673
where Neptune Radar
Unit lost contact.

832
00:25:40,707 --> 00:25:41,775
-Put your shoes on, kids.

833
00:25:41,808 --> 00:25:42,708
We're circling grandma's.

834
00:25:45,245 --> 00:25:48,315
-I wish my life had
meaning. [MONKEY NOISE]

835
00:25:48,348 --> 00:25:52,152
-I can't believe they charge
$3.50 for those things.

836
00:25:52,185 --> 00:25:54,187
-From now on, keep
your eyes peeled.

837
00:25:54,220 --> 00:25:54,820
-Ouch.

838
00:25:54,853 --> 00:25:55,753
-The rocket hit the ocean.

839
00:25:55,787 --> 00:25:56,990
You'll see a dye marker
covering the spot.

840
00:25:57,023 --> 00:25:59,225
I'm going upstairs
so we can get a look.

841
00:25:59,258 --> 00:25:59,892
-Uh, Bob.

842
00:25:59,925 --> 00:26:00,458
This is a plane.

843
00:26:00,492 --> 00:26:01,393
There is no upstairs.

844
00:26:01,427 --> 00:26:05,065
-Meanwhile, over
Bowling Green Tennessee.

845
00:26:05,098 --> 00:26:06,598
-Hey, you ever fly
one of these things?

846
00:26:06,632 --> 00:26:07,267
-Nope.

847
00:26:07,300 --> 00:26:12,305
-Neither have I.

848
00:26:12,338 --> 00:26:15,708
-Hey, I can see my
house from here.

849
00:26:15,742 --> 00:26:17,443
-We're now in the zone where
rocket ran out of fuel.

850
00:26:17,477 --> 00:26:18,410
-This is the pilot.

851
00:26:18,443 --> 00:26:19,912
Anybody back there ever
flown one of these.

852
00:26:19,945 --> 00:26:21,815
-(GERMAN ACCENT) I
know it's my fault.

853
00:26:21,848 --> 00:26:23,817
-I miss the beaver.

854
00:26:23,850 --> 00:26:25,085
-Hey, I can see my
house from here.

855
00:26:25,118 --> 00:26:25,652
-Have you--

856
00:26:25,685 --> 00:26:27,153
-Oh, don't even ask.

857
00:26:27,187 --> 00:26:28,120
-You sure?

858
00:26:28,153 --> 00:26:29,088
-Yeah.

859
00:26:29,122 --> 00:26:31,490
-OK.

860
00:26:31,523 --> 00:26:33,460
-Hm.

861
00:26:33,493 --> 00:26:34,260
-Dead end.

862
00:26:34,293 --> 00:26:35,295
Dead end.

863
00:26:35,328 --> 00:26:36,930
-(SINGING) Dead end,
dead end, dead end,

864
00:26:36,963 --> 00:26:38,498
dead end. [LAUGHS] Sorry.

865
00:26:38,532 --> 00:26:39,198
-Huh?

866
00:26:39,232 --> 00:26:39,732
-What?

867
00:26:42,735 --> 00:26:44,203
-Hm.

868
00:26:44,237 --> 00:26:45,338
-River.

869
00:26:45,372 --> 00:26:46,572
-Island to your left.

870
00:26:46,605 --> 00:26:48,908
I'm swinging over it
so you can take a look.

871
00:26:48,942 --> 00:26:49,975
-Hm.

872
00:26:50,008 --> 00:26:50,675
-His left.

873
00:26:50,708 --> 00:26:52,912
-I can see his scalp from here.

874
00:26:52,945 --> 00:26:54,413
-Cool.

875
00:26:54,447 --> 00:26:55,415
[TRILLING MUSIC]

876
00:26:55,448 --> 00:26:56,415
-Hm?

877
00:26:56,448 --> 00:26:57,348
-Hey.

878
00:26:57,382 --> 00:26:59,318
My-- my watch has
never done that before.

879
00:26:59,352 --> 00:27:01,353
It's trilling.

880
00:27:01,387 --> 00:27:02,355
-Hm.

881
00:27:02,388 --> 00:27:04,923
This place is
crawling with Goldak.

882
00:27:08,762 --> 00:27:10,630
-Rostov, Briggs.

883
00:27:10,663 --> 00:27:11,597
-Rico Youngblood.

884
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:13,232
-The Geiger's jumping
all over the place.

885
00:27:16,035 --> 00:27:17,370
-Meanwhile, over
Three Mile Island.

886
00:27:19,905 --> 00:27:22,042
-Whoa, I can see our
careers from here.

887
00:27:27,313 --> 00:27:28,348
-Right hand red.

888
00:27:31,217 --> 00:27:33,352
-Oh, you ever crashed
one of these before?

889
00:27:33,385 --> 00:27:36,122
-Uh, I'll get coffee.

890
00:27:36,155 --> 00:27:38,257
-I was kidding about
that death pact, Hal.

891
00:27:38,290 --> 00:27:38,790
Come on.

892
00:27:38,823 --> 00:27:40,127
-Hey, what happened?

893
00:27:40,160 --> 00:27:41,427
-We're losing altitude fast.

894
00:27:41,460 --> 00:27:42,928
-Hey, don't get
technical with me.

895
00:27:42,962 --> 00:27:44,663
-All the magnetic
controls are conked out.

896
00:27:44,697 --> 00:27:46,332
The electrical system's shot.

897
00:27:46,365 --> 00:27:48,100
Whole panel is dead, Joe.

898
00:27:48,133 --> 00:27:50,670
As dead as we'll be if you don't
ditch this baby and bail out.

899
00:27:50,703 --> 00:27:51,603
-No, we'll be deader.

900
00:27:51,637 --> 00:27:52,705
- --and take care of the others.

901
00:27:52,738 --> 00:27:53,940
I'm going to try to
make that island.

902
00:27:53,973 --> 00:27:55,175
-Yeah, I'll go get coffee.

903
00:27:58,010 --> 00:28:00,447
-The captain has put
on No Smoking sign.

904
00:28:00,480 --> 00:28:03,483
Your seat back doubles
as a flotation device.

905
00:28:03,517 --> 00:28:04,283
-You look good.

906
00:28:04,317 --> 00:28:06,218
And you Adolf--
you look fabulous.

907
00:28:09,688 --> 00:28:13,125
-Help me!

908
00:28:13,158 --> 00:28:15,128
-You ever dive one
of these things?

909
00:28:15,162 --> 00:28:18,063
-We blew it, Phil.

910
00:28:18,097 --> 00:28:18,598
Damn fly.

911
00:28:21,835 --> 00:28:23,235
[MONKEY NOISES]

912
00:28:23,268 --> 00:28:25,105
-I'm only lending
[INAUDIBLE], Major.

913
00:28:25,138 --> 00:28:26,505
Bring it back to
me in one piece.

914
00:28:26,538 --> 00:28:27,072
-Can't you tell?

915
00:28:27,105 --> 00:28:28,107
I want to die with you.

916
00:28:28,140 --> 00:28:28,640
-Be kind.

917
00:28:28,673 --> 00:28:29,575
Be gentle with her.

918
00:28:29,608 --> 00:28:32,512
She won't double cross you.

919
00:28:32,545 --> 00:28:33,547
Easy, Major.

920
00:28:37,450 --> 00:28:39,385
-Oh, that looks
like a good place.

921
00:28:39,418 --> 00:28:41,620
-Oh, where's George
Kennedy when you need him?

922
00:28:41,653 --> 00:28:43,222
-You ever crash one
of these things?

923
00:28:43,255 --> 00:28:53,065
-[CLEARS_THROAT]

924
00:28:53,098 --> 00:28:57,003
-Thank you for flying Northwest.

925
00:28:57,037 --> 00:28:58,905
-Hey, we landed on a witch.

926
00:28:58,938 --> 00:29:00,607
Maybe the film will be
in color from this point.

927
00:29:04,877 --> 00:29:05,612
-All right, you kids.

928
00:29:05,645 --> 00:29:06,947
Quit horsing around back there.

929
00:29:06,980 --> 00:29:08,648
-Well, it's time to let
you play in-flight Twister.

930
00:29:08,682 --> 00:29:09,448
-Woo-hoo.

931
00:29:09,482 --> 00:29:10,917
-It smells like a
wrestling match.

932
00:29:10,950 --> 00:29:11,483
-Yo.

933
00:29:11,517 --> 00:29:12,185
-Get the first aid kit.

934
00:29:12,218 --> 00:29:13,318
Briggs is bleeding.

935
00:29:13,352 --> 00:29:14,820
-You did a beautiful job, Major.

936
00:29:14,853 --> 00:29:16,055
-I didn't think we had a chance.

937
00:29:16,088 --> 00:29:18,692
-Let's form a soccer
team and eat each other.

938
00:29:18,725 --> 00:29:19,725
-This will hurt for a minute.

939
00:29:19,758 --> 00:29:21,493
-Yeah, that's what I
always tell my kids.

940
00:29:21,527 --> 00:29:22,195
-Ow, ow.

941
00:29:22,228 --> 00:29:23,262
-What went wrong?

942
00:29:23,295 --> 00:29:24,697
-Maybe there was hand soap
in the hydraulic fluid.

943
00:29:24,730 --> 00:29:26,498
Magnetic instruments,
the radio, everything--

944
00:29:26,532 --> 00:29:28,100
they just stopped cold.

945
00:29:28,133 --> 00:29:28,767
-That's strange.

946
00:29:28,800 --> 00:29:29,668
-I usually have a line here.

947
00:29:29,702 --> 00:29:30,837
-Just before we
crashed, the Geiger

948
00:29:30,870 --> 00:29:32,272
registered intense
radio activity.

949
00:29:32,305 --> 00:29:32,772
-Hm.

950
00:29:32,805 --> 00:29:33,272
-Oh.

951
00:29:33,305 --> 00:29:34,540
-Huh?

952
00:29:34,573 --> 00:29:35,508
-What do you make of that?

953
00:29:35,542 --> 00:29:36,175
-[INAUDIBLE].

954
00:29:36,208 --> 00:29:36,808
Oh.

955
00:29:36,842 --> 00:29:37,877
-We're over something hot.

956
00:29:37,910 --> 00:29:39,845
Other than that, your
guess is as good as mine.

957
00:29:39,878 --> 00:29:40,713
-Oh, whoa!

958
00:29:40,747 --> 00:29:41,547
-Get the Geiger.

959
00:29:41,580 --> 00:29:42,882
Whoa!

960
00:29:42,915 --> 00:29:45,150
-Lot of Goldak reading here.

961
00:29:45,183 --> 00:29:46,318
Oh, thank goodness.

962
00:29:46,352 --> 00:29:48,320
-It sounds like an
old Duran Duran tape.

963
00:29:48,353 --> 00:29:49,288
-Oh, "Hungry Like the Wolf?"

964
00:29:49,322 --> 00:29:52,125
-Yeah, you got it.

965
00:29:52,158 --> 00:29:53,793
-This is an old movie.

966
00:29:53,827 --> 00:29:55,195
-Oh, no Goldak here.

967
00:29:55,228 --> 00:29:57,763
-I can see you're really
upset about this, Dave.

968
00:29:57,797 --> 00:29:59,198
-But there's nothing.

969
00:29:59,232 --> 00:30:00,333
-Hey, my watch is going.

970
00:30:00,367 --> 00:30:01,733
It stopped before,
but now it's going.

971
00:30:01,767 --> 00:30:02,300
-Great.

972
00:30:02,333 --> 00:30:03,535
I'll alert the media.

973
00:30:03,568 --> 00:30:04,737
-Let's go out and take a look
at this South Sea paradise.

974
00:30:04,770 --> 00:30:06,372
-You'll be all right, Briggs.

975
00:30:06,405 --> 00:30:07,207
-Yeah, what a joker.

976
00:30:12,412 --> 00:30:13,445
-Ah, just put that anywhere.

977
00:30:15,982 --> 00:30:17,717
-Oh, what a day.

978
00:30:17,750 --> 00:30:19,218
-Yeah, you left the
morning paper here.

979
00:30:19,252 --> 00:30:19,718
-Beautiful.

980
00:30:19,752 --> 00:30:20,220
-What a place.

981
00:30:20,253 --> 00:30:20,820
-Good morning.

982
00:30:20,853 --> 00:30:21,387
-Morning.

983
00:30:21,420 --> 00:30:21,953
-Morning.

984
00:30:21,987 --> 00:30:23,255
-Seems rather peaceful.

985
00:30:23,288 --> 00:30:25,692
-Talked to some of our guys
who crashed in New Guinea

986
00:30:25,725 --> 00:30:26,392
during the war.

987
00:30:26,425 --> 00:30:27,025
-Whoa.

988
00:30:27,058 --> 00:30:29,062
I'm sorry I brought it up.

989
00:30:29,095 --> 00:30:31,030
-There may be a park, but I'm
not going to depend on it.

990
00:30:31,063 --> 00:30:32,898
-Hey, maybe we oughta try to
get the radio working, huh?

991
00:30:32,932 --> 00:30:33,933
Let somebody know where we are?

992
00:30:33,967 --> 00:30:36,102
-Hey, maybe it's time to
take that parachute off.

993
00:30:36,135 --> 00:30:37,070
- --until we find the rocket.

994
00:30:37,103 --> 00:30:38,103
-Oh, that's just fine.

995
00:30:38,137 --> 00:30:39,505
Head hunters, cannibals.

996
00:30:39,538 --> 00:30:40,673
-Break out the guns
and ammunition.

997
00:30:40,707 --> 00:30:42,742
-It's time for a celebration!

998
00:30:42,775 --> 00:30:43,542
-Yee-haw.

999
00:30:43,575 --> 00:30:44,343
-Better check all
your equipment.

1000
00:30:44,377 --> 00:30:46,947
Take only the essential things.

1001
00:30:46,980 --> 00:30:47,947
-Like Vitalis.

1002
00:30:47,980 --> 00:30:49,448
-Looks like we might be
camping out for a while.

1003
00:30:49,482 --> 00:30:50,783
-Night falls quickly
in the jungle.

1004
00:30:50,817 --> 00:30:51,483
-Whoa.

1005
00:30:51,517 --> 00:30:53,018
-But morning comes just as soon.

1006
00:30:53,052 --> 00:30:55,388
-Guys, this kind of reminds
me of a movie I once saw,

1007
00:30:55,422 --> 00:30:57,923
about two guys who crashed
in a jungle on the--

1008
00:30:57,957 --> 00:30:59,425
-I wish we could have
a charbroiled hamburger

1009
00:30:59,458 --> 00:31:00,593
sandwich and some french fried.

1010
00:31:00,627 --> 00:31:01,393
Potatoes.

1011
00:31:01,427 --> 00:31:02,462
-Oh, that's what
I'm talking about.

1012
00:31:02,495 --> 00:31:02,962
-Heh, heh.

1013
00:31:02,995 --> 00:31:05,732
Yeah!

1014
00:31:05,765 --> 00:31:08,333
-Oops, there goes another
rubber tree plant.

1015
00:31:08,367 --> 00:31:10,603
-Thanks, Frank.

1016
00:31:10,637 --> 00:31:12,305
-Hey, are they
walking in a circle?

1017
00:31:15,375 --> 00:31:16,208
-Ah.

1018
00:31:16,242 --> 00:31:18,543
-Hey, I hope
there's a gift shop.

1019
00:31:18,577 --> 00:31:20,780
-What do you think?

1020
00:31:20,813 --> 00:31:21,713
-Who knows?

1021
00:31:21,747 --> 00:31:23,348
Maybe we're being
set up for pigeons.

1022
00:31:23,382 --> 00:31:25,250
-And you're the mother-loving
pigeon of them all.

1023
00:31:25,283 --> 00:31:27,453
[COOING]

1024
00:31:27,487 --> 00:31:29,088
-Well, just remember
the rule, everybody.

1025
00:31:29,122 --> 00:31:31,057
If you don't understand
it, shoot it.

1026
00:31:31,090 --> 00:31:34,027
-Look for a woman named Wanama.

1027
00:31:34,060 --> 00:31:35,895
-Gilligan?

1028
00:31:35,928 --> 00:31:38,563
-Mary Anne?

1029
00:31:38,597 --> 00:31:39,332
-Torch the village.

1030
00:31:39,365 --> 00:31:40,800
Let's do the whole village, man.

1031
00:31:40,833 --> 00:31:41,733
Let's do it!

1032
00:31:47,707 --> 00:31:48,573
-It's a Gauguin still life!

1033
00:31:48,607 --> 00:31:51,510
Shoot it!

1034
00:31:51,543 --> 00:31:52,812
-Hey.

1035
00:31:52,845 --> 00:31:57,417
Beaver, June, what
are you doing here?

1036
00:31:57,450 --> 00:31:59,352
-This ain't gonna
be such a bad leave.

1037
00:31:59,385 --> 00:32:01,653
-It's perfect.

1038
00:32:01,687 --> 00:32:04,457
Boy, not bad.

1039
00:32:04,490 --> 00:32:05,623
-[CLEARS_THROAT] Scuze me.

1040
00:32:05,657 --> 00:32:09,428
You know how to get back to
the interstate from here?

1041
00:32:09,462 --> 00:32:10,430
-All right.

1042
00:32:10,463 --> 00:32:13,232
Jim, Mack, Joe, Fred, Michael.

1043
00:32:13,265 --> 00:32:15,000
-Too bad we don't
know their language.

1044
00:32:15,033 --> 00:32:16,802
-Yeah, she might
be able to help us.

1045
00:32:16,835 --> 00:32:18,303
-I know a couple of
Melanesian dialects.

1046
00:32:18,337 --> 00:32:19,272
I'll try them out on her.

1047
00:32:19,305 --> 00:32:19,772
-Who are you?

1048
00:32:19,805 --> 00:32:20,272
-Whoa!

1049
00:32:20,305 --> 00:32:21,240
Don't do that.

1050
00:32:21,273 --> 00:32:22,342
-You know English.

1051
00:32:22,375 --> 00:32:24,043
-From missionary school.

1052
00:32:24,077 --> 00:32:26,445
Island over there,
where my people go.

1053
00:32:26,478 --> 00:32:27,147
-It's called Harvard.

1054
00:32:27,180 --> 00:32:27,947
-You're people left?

1055
00:32:27,980 --> 00:32:28,748
-Why?

1056
00:32:28,782 --> 00:32:30,683
-Fire bird fly over village.

