00:58:03,413 --> 00:58:05,350
-Or ever will be, major.

1845
00:58:05,383 --> 00:58:06,717
-You're looking
at a kind of world

1846
00:58:06,750 --> 00:58:08,620
that hasn't existed
for millions of years.

1847
00:58:08,653 --> 00:58:09,553
-Who are you, Camus?

1848
00:58:09,587 --> 00:58:10,755
-Sort of like a--
like a lost continent.

1849
00:58:10,788 --> 00:58:11,923
-Ah, you could call
it that I guess.

1850
00:58:11,957 --> 00:58:13,592
-Hey, good name.

1851
00:58:13,625 --> 00:58:14,558
-Hm.

1852
00:58:14,592 --> 00:58:16,827
-Climb a mountain in
the middle of the sky.

1853
00:58:16,860 --> 00:58:17,328
-Heavy.

1854
00:58:17,362 --> 00:58:18,428
-Volcanic dust.

1855
00:58:18,462 --> 00:58:19,630
-Came out of my pant leg.

1856
00:58:19,663 --> 00:58:21,398
-It's almost as if
time forgot this place.

1857
00:58:21,432 --> 00:58:24,102
-Everything indicates a
throwback to prehistoric times.

1858
00:58:24,135 --> 00:58:24,902
Look at that foliage.

1859
00:58:24,935 --> 00:58:25,537
-You look at it.

1860
00:58:25,570 --> 00:58:26,670
I'm bitter.

1861
00:58:26,703 --> 00:58:28,940
- --tremendous internal
pressures causing such steamy

1862
00:58:28,973 --> 00:58:29,740
pools.

1863
00:58:29,773 --> 00:58:31,342
-I told you it was
the Cobb salad.

1864
00:58:31,375 --> 00:58:32,343
- --activity at such altitude.

1865
00:58:32,377 --> 00:58:34,245
-Must be terrific.

1866
00:58:34,278 --> 00:58:36,613
As powerful as a stockpile
of hydrogen bombs.

1867
00:58:36,647 --> 00:58:38,082
-Or a stockpile
of stock footage.

1868
00:58:38,115 --> 00:58:40,050
-Yeah, but it
doesn't make sense.

1869
00:58:40,083 --> 00:58:41,785
-The air is thin,
sure, but you can

1870
00:58:41,818 --> 00:58:43,420
breathe without your
lungs screaming for help.

1871
00:58:43,453 --> 00:58:45,423
-We're screaming for help.

1872
00:58:45,457 --> 00:58:47,258
-It's all the plants, Major.

1873
00:58:47,292 --> 00:58:48,760
Practically pure oxygen.

1874
00:58:48,793 --> 00:58:49,860
-But look at this haze.

1875
00:58:49,893 --> 00:58:50,827
-Purple haze.

1876
00:58:50,860 --> 00:58:52,230
-Somebody got lonesome
for some soft light,

1877
00:58:52,263 --> 00:58:54,232
put a green bulb in the sun.

1878
00:58:54,265 --> 00:58:55,900
-Yeah, like in a nightclub.

1879
00:58:55,933 --> 00:58:57,768
Only they forgot to
bring on the dames, huh?

1880
00:58:57,802 --> 00:58:59,002
-Thank you, Morty Gunty.

1881
00:58:59,035 --> 00:59:01,705
-We know what the atom bomb
did to the atmosphere, Major.

1882
00:59:01,738 --> 00:59:03,207
The same forces
could exist here.

1883
00:59:03,240 --> 00:59:06,377
-Uh, I think his accent has
gotten thinner with the air.

1884
00:59:06,410 --> 00:59:08,578
-If we find that rocket,
it may have us someday

1885
00:59:08,612 --> 00:59:12,183
all living back in a
world like this one.

1886
00:59:12,217 --> 00:59:14,052
-I'll worry about that
when the time comes.

1887
00:59:14,085 --> 00:59:16,053
-God know we've got tons
of that lying around.

1888
00:59:16,087 --> 00:59:17,922
-Hey Phillips, take a fix.

1889
00:59:17,955 --> 00:59:19,823
See if we can pick up a reading
on the rocket somewhere.

1890
00:59:19,857 --> 00:59:20,692
-Oh, the rocket.

1891
00:59:20,725 --> 00:59:21,358
Thank you.

1892
00:59:21,392 --> 00:59:22,560
I forgot what we were doing.

1893
00:59:22,593 --> 00:59:23,093
-Yeah.

1894
00:59:25,830 --> 00:59:28,032
-Well, that was when I was 14.

1895
00:59:28,065 --> 00:59:29,400
Now, later that summer, I--

1896
00:59:29,433 --> 00:59:30,468
-Tell me something, Joe.

1897
00:59:30,502 --> 00:59:31,202
What's eating you?

1898
00:59:31,235 --> 00:59:31,902
-Chili peppers.

1899
00:59:31,935 --> 00:59:33,672
I burned my gut.

1900
00:59:33,705 --> 00:59:35,540
-Privately, what is it?

1901
00:59:35,573 --> 00:59:37,575
Drop in this little dream world.

1902
00:59:37,608 --> 00:59:38,542
-Maybe.

1903
00:59:38,575 --> 00:59:41,078
-Maybe not.

1904
00:59:41,112 --> 00:59:42,547
-What do you think of Rostov?

1905
00:59:42,580 --> 00:59:43,047
-I like him.

1906
00:59:43,080 --> 00:59:43,580
He's neat.

1907
00:59:43,613 --> 00:59:44,447
-Rostov?

1908
00:59:44,480 --> 00:59:46,050
-I can light a
cigarette, maybe if I--

1909
00:59:46,083 --> 00:59:47,518
-He's a cold fish.

1910
00:59:47,552 --> 00:59:49,153
You know those big brains.

1911
00:59:49,187 --> 00:59:51,822
-No, can't say as I do.

1912
00:59:51,855 --> 00:59:53,623
-Oh.

1913
00:59:53,657 --> 00:59:54,625
I see.

1914
00:59:54,658 --> 00:59:55,827
You think that he--

1915
00:59:55,860 --> 00:59:57,195
-I don't think anything.

1916
00:59:57,228 --> 00:59:59,497
-Well, we know that.

1917
00:59:59,530 --> 01:00:02,667
-I never did buy that guy,
even before we took off.

1918
01:00:02,700 --> 01:00:04,068
-Well, the producer did.

1919
01:00:04,102 --> 01:00:06,803
-Do we know anything
about the rocket?

1920
01:00:06,837 --> 01:00:07,805
Willie?

1921
01:00:07,838 --> 01:00:10,240
No.

1922
01:00:10,273 --> 01:00:12,343
Only three guys know
what makes it work.

1923
01:00:12,377 --> 01:00:13,778
-And none of them
are in this film.

1924
01:00:13,812 --> 01:00:15,145
-Rostov, Phillips, and Briggs.

1925
01:00:15,178 --> 01:00:16,080
-What about Hunga Dunga?

1926
01:00:16,113 --> 01:00:17,282
-Oh, you left him out.

1927
01:00:17,315 --> 01:00:18,750
And he's the most
important one, too.

1928
01:00:18,783 --> 01:00:20,552
-Anything happens to Phillips,
that just leaves Rostov.

1929
01:00:20,585 --> 01:00:23,587
He could sell us
any bill of goods.

1930
01:00:23,620 --> 01:00:25,923
Maybe he didn't want
that rocket to turn back.

1931
01:00:25,957 --> 01:00:27,558
-Hm.

1932
01:00:27,592 --> 01:00:29,860
-Rough.

1933
01:00:29,893 --> 01:00:31,028
-See what you make of this.

1934
01:00:31,062 --> 01:00:32,430
-Oh, earmuffs I guess.

1935
01:00:32,463 --> 01:00:33,832
-Hey Major, we
picked something up.

1936
01:00:33,865 --> 01:00:37,235
-Well, let's hope it's a
virus and it kills them all.

1937
01:00:37,268 --> 01:00:38,737
-That's it, Phillips!

1938
01:00:38,770 --> 01:00:39,637
-What's the rundown?

1939
01:00:39,670 --> 01:00:42,040
-Definite signs
of radioactivity.

1940
01:00:42,073 --> 01:00:44,475
Much too powerful to be coming
from the unit in the rocket.

1941
01:00:44,508 --> 01:00:46,243
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

1942
01:00:46,277 --> 01:00:48,612
-We may have hit something
important, Major.

1943
01:00:48,645 --> 01:00:49,447
-Like the plot?

1944
01:00:49,480 --> 01:00:50,080
-Uranium.

1945
01:00:50,113 --> 01:00:51,315
-Please say it's the plot.

1946
01:00:51,348 --> 01:00:52,415
I hope it's the plot.

1947
01:00:52,448 --> 01:00:53,350
-Explains a lot of things--
why the plane went out

1948
01:00:53,383 --> 01:00:54,652
of control when we
flew toward the island.

1949
01:00:54,685 --> 01:00:56,387
-Why domestic comedies
are so successful.

1950
01:00:56,420 --> 01:00:58,422
-We must have entered
a field of radiation.

1951
01:00:58,455 --> 01:01:00,858
And then we dropped below the
mountain, we passed out of it.

1952
01:01:00,892 --> 01:01:02,058
It lost its effect.

1953
01:01:02,092 --> 01:01:05,328
-Why couldn't you have told
us this 20 minutes ago, you?

1954
01:01:05,362 --> 01:01:07,298
-Does that stuff
act like a magnet?

1955
01:01:07,332 --> 01:01:10,100
-That stuff is the most
mysterious element in nature.

1956
01:01:10,133 --> 01:01:10,633
-Love?

1957
01:01:10,667 --> 01:01:11,535
-The most dangerous.

1958
01:01:11,568 --> 01:01:12,503
-(GERMAN ACCENT) It's potent.

1959
01:01:12,537 --> 01:01:14,472
-The most unstable,
the most valuable.

1960
01:01:14,505 --> 01:01:16,173
-Yeah, babies cry for it.

1961
01:01:16,207 --> 01:01:16,673
-Huh?

1962
01:01:16,707 --> 01:01:17,340
-Huh?

1963
01:01:17,373 --> 01:01:19,643
I think he lost you, Cesar.

1964
01:01:19,677 --> 01:01:21,145
-(GERMAN ACCENT) Keep
my uranium out of this.

1965
01:01:21,178 --> 01:01:22,747
- --ran out of fuel, it was
inevitably drawn to these

1966
01:01:22,780 --> 01:01:23,313
fields.

1967
01:01:23,347 --> 01:01:23,847
If

1968
01:01:23,880 --> 01:01:24,815
-It ran out of fuel

1969
01:01:24,848 --> 01:01:26,150
-Don't ever say that.

1970
01:01:26,183 --> 01:01:26,883
-Simmer down, Joe.

1971
01:01:26,917 --> 01:01:29,187
-So I'll apologize if I'm wrong.

1972
01:01:29,220 --> 01:01:30,220
-Meh.

1973
01:01:30,253 --> 01:01:30,955
-All right.

1974
01:01:30,988 --> 01:01:31,888
It's the only led we have.

1975
01:01:31,922 --> 01:01:33,390
Let's follow it.

1976
01:01:33,423 --> 01:01:34,792
-That square bugs me.

1977
01:01:34,825 --> 01:01:35,827
He really bugs me!

1978
01:01:35,860 --> 01:01:36,760
-Easy, easy.

1979
01:01:39,530 --> 01:01:40,998
-Rules of the road, boys.

1980
01:01:41,032 --> 01:01:42,900
See anything, shoot to kill.

1981
01:01:42,933 --> 01:01:44,302
I mean, don't shoot
it if it's going

1982
01:01:44,335 --> 01:01:46,137
to advance the plot, though.

1983
01:01:46,170 --> 01:01:47,638
-Saigon.

1984
01:01:47,672 --> 01:01:50,407
I can't believe I'm in Saigon.

1985
01:01:50,440 --> 01:01:51,675
-Did you get him, Sergeant?

1986
01:01:51,708 --> 01:01:54,378
-Oh please let him
get malaria, please.

1987
01:01:54,412 --> 01:01:56,813
- --for four hours like I was
something sent up from room

1988
01:01:56,847 --> 01:01:57,315
service.

1989
01:01:57,348 --> 01:01:58,748
-[RIMSHOT]

1990
01:01:58,782 --> 01:02:01,085
-This movie makes "Shoah"
look like a two-reeler.

1991
01:02:01,118 --> 01:02:02,285
-Easily.

1992
01:02:02,318 --> 01:02:04,288
-Think of any late news flashes
from the home front, Doc?

1993
01:02:04,322 --> 01:02:05,155
-Huh?

1994
01:02:05,188 --> 01:02:06,857
-How's everybody
at the Pentagon?

1995
01:02:06,890 --> 01:02:07,725
Happy?

1996
01:02:07,758 --> 01:02:09,660
-What did you say?

1997
01:02:09,693 --> 01:02:10,327
-Oh, nothing.

1998
01:02:10,360 --> 01:02:11,293
I just wondered--

1999
01:02:11,327 --> 01:02:14,398
-I just thought I'd
sneak a few lines in.

2000
01:02:14,432 --> 01:02:16,100
-Oh, well, from
this activity, we're

2001
01:02:16,133 --> 01:02:17,635
moving into a hotter zone.

2002
01:02:17,668 --> 01:02:20,472
-Well, at least we
won't freeze tonight.

2003
01:02:20,505 --> 01:02:22,072
-You coming on to me?

2004
01:02:22,105 --> 01:02:23,573
-And there are on
the handle was a--

2005
01:02:23,607 --> 01:02:25,208
-Well, if that rocket is
near the uranium fields,

2006
01:02:25,242 --> 01:02:26,477
we're getting pretty close.

2007
01:02:26,510 --> 01:02:29,213
I show a concentration
of almost 20,000 MRs.

2008
01:02:29,247 --> 01:02:30,080
-MRs?
-MRs?

2009
01:02:30,113 --> 01:02:31,180
-Like a handful of yurs?

2010
01:02:31,213 --> 01:02:33,050
-How about a short translate
for the common man, Doc?

2011
01:02:33,083 --> 01:02:33,617
-Go scratch.

2012
01:02:33,650 --> 01:02:35,452
Is that short enough?

2013
01:02:35,485 --> 01:02:36,452
-Billy who?

2014
01:02:36,485 --> 01:02:37,688
-It's like taking your
temperature, Sergeant.

2015
01:02:37,722 --> 01:02:38,855
-Yeah, now bend over.

2016
01:02:38,888 --> 01:02:40,992
-Only this shows how hot a
zone is with radioactivity.

2017
01:02:41,025 --> 01:02:43,060
-How dangerous is the area?

2018
01:02:43,093 --> 01:02:43,828
-Don't worry, Major.

2019
01:02:43,862 --> 01:02:44,895
We're safe enough.

2020
01:02:44,928 --> 01:02:47,498
But if the uranium in
those fields were refined,

2021
01:02:47,532 --> 01:02:48,833
we'd have been dead hours ago.

2022
01:02:48,867 --> 01:02:50,635
-The Japanese would have
owned all this anyway.

2023
01:02:50,668 --> 01:02:51,968
-Raise your flaps.

2024
01:02:52,002 --> 01:02:54,638
That rocket won't find us.

2025
01:02:54,672 --> 01:02:56,407
-You've been sarcastic
this whole film.

2026
01:02:56,440 --> 01:02:57,975
Is something wrong?

2027
01:02:58,008 --> 01:02:59,477
-I joined the Air Force.

2028
01:03:02,280 --> 01:03:03,647
-Oh, fun.

2029
01:03:03,680 --> 01:03:04,180
-Yeah.

2030
01:03:04,213 --> 01:03:05,917
[SARCASTIC LAUGHTER]

2031
01:03:05,950 --> 01:03:07,852
-Ah, let's take a
15-minute break.

2032
01:03:07,885 --> 01:03:09,120
-Oh, but keep the
camera running.

2033
01:03:09,153 --> 01:03:11,355
[GROANING]

2034
01:03:11,388 --> 01:03:13,290
-Break out the rations, Sarge.

2035
01:03:13,323 --> 01:03:14,825
-They take a lot of
breaks, those guys.

2036
01:03:18,262 --> 01:03:20,497
-Ah, tortellini
with pesto sauce.

2037
01:03:20,530 --> 01:03:21,865
-I've got radicchio.

2038
01:03:21,898 --> 01:03:22,933
-I've got haggis.

2039
01:03:22,967 --> 01:03:24,268
-This sauce is delicious.

2040
01:03:24,302 --> 01:03:25,268
It's so light.

2041
01:03:25,302 --> 01:03:27,738
Something tastes like
cilantro, only milder.

2042
01:03:27,772 --> 01:03:29,840
My compliments to Dinty Moore.

2043
01:03:29,873 --> 01:03:32,243
-Mm-hm.

2044
01:03:32,277 --> 01:03:36,013
-This is so good, I've
got to go tell someone.

2045
01:03:39,450 --> 01:03:40,417
-Hey.

2046
01:03:40,450 --> 01:03:41,718
Who booted over here?

2047
01:03:41,752 --> 01:03:42,285
[LAUGHTER]

2048
01:03:42,318 --> 01:03:43,653
-Hey, we've got company.

2049
01:03:43,687 --> 01:03:44,322
-All right.

2050
01:03:44,355 --> 01:03:44,988
Clean this place up.

2051
01:03:45,022 --> 01:03:47,023
We've got company.

2052
01:03:47,057 --> 01:03:48,893
-Yuck.

2053
01:03:48,927 --> 01:03:50,527
Ew.

2054
01:03:50,560 --> 01:03:52,195
-Carrots and stuff.

2055
01:03:52,228 --> 01:03:53,230
-What is that?

2056
01:03:53,263 --> 01:03:54,732
-These tracks are fresh.

2057
01:03:54,765 --> 01:03:56,033
-Mountain cows.

2058
01:03:56,067 --> 01:03:56,633
Nothing scarier.

2059
01:03:56,667 --> 01:03:57,335
-Fine, they're fresh.

2060
01:03:57,368 --> 01:03:59,070
But what do they fit?

2061
01:03:59,103 --> 01:04:00,403
-Nothing human eye ever saw.

2062
01:04:00,437 --> 01:04:02,373
Not even my top sergeant
had feet that big.

2063
01:04:02,407 --> 01:04:03,240
-Hm.

2064
01:04:03,273 --> 01:04:04,675
-I've seen tracks
like these before.

2065
01:04:04,708 --> 01:04:05,508
-Yeah, where?

2066
01:04:05,542 --> 01:04:06,310
-Larry Mondello.

2067
01:04:06,343 --> 01:04:08,178
-In a museum.

2068
01:04:08,212 --> 01:04:10,013
-[INAUDIBLE] Museum
in Citrusdal.

2069
01:04:10,047 --> 01:04:10,947
Rode one.

2070
01:04:10,980 --> 01:04:12,250
It almost bucked me
off, but I took him.

2071
01:04:12,283 --> 01:04:13,050
-Good for you.

2072
01:04:13,083 --> 01:04:15,552
-Hard to believe,
isn't it, Major?

2073
01:04:15,585 --> 01:04:19,523
-My calendar says 20th
century AD not 100 million BC.

2074
01:04:19,557 --> 01:04:21,092
-Yeah, but you've got
a Chippendale calendar.

2075
01:04:21,125 --> 01:04:22,260
-I appreciate your
feelings, Major,

2076
01:04:22,293 --> 01:04:24,395
but you can't change
what we've seen.

2077
01:04:24,428 --> 01:04:25,697
-I know, I know.

2078
01:04:28,165 --> 01:04:28,732
-Film.
-Come on.

2079
01:04:28,765 --> 01:04:29,433
Let's get our packs.

2080
01:04:29,467 --> 01:04:30,968
We've got to move.

2081
01:04:31,002 --> 01:04:32,837
-Oh, can I finish my yogurt?

2082
01:04:32,870 --> 01:04:33,603
It's really delicious.

2083
01:04:33,637 --> 01:04:35,305
It's got--

2084
01:04:35,338 --> 01:04:37,275
-Ah, now this is where
they shot "Mad Monster."

2085
01:04:37,308 --> 01:04:38,575
In a little ways
down here, you'll

2086
01:04:38,608 --> 01:04:40,043
see where they did
"Jungle Goddess."

2087
01:04:40,077 --> 01:04:42,713
There's "Rocket Ship XM,"
filmed right here too.

2088
01:04:42,747 --> 01:04:43,580
-Oh, neat.

2089
01:04:43,613 --> 01:04:44,548
-I'll make another
check if you like.

2090
01:04:44,582 --> 01:04:46,417
-Oh, that'll take 10 minutes.

2091
01:04:46,450 --> 01:04:46,950
-Oh.

2092
01:04:53,657 --> 01:04:55,627
-Would you put that
thing down, please?

2093
01:04:58,295 --> 01:04:58,963
-All right.

2094
01:04:58,997 --> 01:04:59,997
On course.

2095
01:05:00,030 --> 01:05:00,497
-Good.

2096
01:05:00,530 --> 01:05:01,430
-Yeah.

2097
01:05:01,463 --> 01:05:05,035
You sure that thing
really works, Hugh?

2098
01:05:05,068 --> 01:05:06,570
-Hey, Doc.

2099
01:05:06,603 --> 01:05:07,772
More bronto-- more tracks.

2100
01:05:07,805 --> 01:05:08,938
-Bronto Ham [INAUDIBLE]

2101
01:05:08,972 --> 01:05:12,208
-[INAUDIBLE] and the tracks
and the [INAUDIBLE] over.

2102
01:05:12,242 --> 01:05:13,943
-Hey, Doc.

2103
01:05:13,977 --> 01:05:17,247
Think there's some other way to
get those uranium fields, huh?

2104
01:05:17,280 --> 01:05:18,513
