01:13:41,785 --> 01:13:43,720
-I wonder how he keeps
his hair back like that.

2367
01:13:43,753 --> 01:13:45,355
He kind of looks
like Patti LaBelle.

2368
01:13:45,388 --> 01:13:48,025
-You and me, going round
and round, model a model.

2369
01:13:48,058 --> 01:13:48,892
-Hurts, don't it?

2370
01:13:48,925 --> 01:13:49,525
Here's your mail.

2371
01:13:49,558 --> 01:13:51,228
Oops, forgot to stamp it.

2372
01:13:51,262 --> 01:13:52,395
-Sergeant, keep your eye on him.

2373
01:13:52,428 --> 01:13:54,898
-Start plotting those spitballs.

2374
01:13:54,932 --> 01:13:56,900
-Yeah.

2375
01:13:56,933 --> 01:13:59,102
-Gee, Dad, why'd you have to
get a green-stick fracture.

2376
01:13:59,135 --> 01:14:00,903
-I'm eating you up now.

2377
01:14:00,937 --> 01:14:01,838
Heh, how's the farm?

2378
01:14:01,872 --> 01:14:02,738
Here's a couple more acres.

2379
01:14:02,772 --> 01:14:06,008
-You got him.

2380
01:14:06,042 --> 01:14:07,477
[SCREECHING]

2381
01:14:07,510 --> 01:14:10,913
-Just as dinosaurs must
protect their young,

2382
01:14:10,947 --> 01:14:14,050
so must you invest
in Mutual of Omaha.

2383
01:14:14,083 --> 01:14:15,485
-Help Danny get him out.

2384
01:14:20,623 --> 01:14:21,225
-Eat it, boy.

2385
01:14:21,258 --> 01:14:22,025
Eat it.

2386
01:14:22,058 --> 01:14:22,525
Arr.

2387
01:14:22,558 --> 01:14:23,493
-Oh, oh, ow.

2388
01:14:23,527 --> 01:14:24,327
You got my braces.

2389
01:14:24,360 --> 01:14:25,295
Ow, owie.

2390
01:14:32,768 --> 01:14:33,237
-They're gone.

2391
01:14:33,270 --> 01:14:34,838
We can quit now.

2392
01:14:34,872 --> 01:14:36,773
Let's go.

2393
01:14:36,807 --> 01:14:37,540
-Say uncle.

2394
01:14:37,573 --> 01:14:38,408
-Dinasure.

2395
01:14:38,442 --> 01:14:40,910
-Aw, but this is
the coolest part.

2396
01:14:56,660 --> 01:14:59,797
-Help me, I'm about to fall.

2397
01:14:59,830 --> 01:15:01,498
You've got to help me!

2398
01:15:01,532 --> 01:15:04,602
I'm pulling my arm
out of the socket.

2399
01:15:04,635 --> 01:15:06,503
Don't grab by butt.

2400
01:15:06,537 --> 01:15:07,670
Ah!

2401
01:15:07,703 --> 01:15:08,572
TOM (OFFSCREEN): Hey, Joel.

2402
01:15:08,605 --> 01:15:09,807
Come over here and look at this.

2403
01:15:09,840 --> 01:15:11,442
CROW (OFFSCREEN): Yeah,
this is really cool.

2404
01:15:11,475 --> 01:15:11,975
-Hey, Cambot.

2405
01:15:12,008 --> 01:15:12,775
Follow me over here.

2406
01:15:12,808 --> 01:15:14,010
CROW (OFFSCREEN): Wow, yeah.

2407
01:15:14,043 --> 01:15:15,245
-Wow, that thing's super neat.

2408
01:15:15,278 --> 01:15:15,812
-Cool, huh?

2409
01:15:15,845 --> 01:15:16,813
-Oh, just wait a minute.

2410
01:15:16,847 --> 01:15:17,347
Wait.

2411
01:15:17,380 --> 01:15:18,048
It's coming around.

2412
01:15:18,082 --> 01:15:18,548
There.

2413
01:15:18,582 --> 01:15:19,048
-Whoa.

2414
01:15:19,082 --> 01:15:20,117
-Oh, man.

2415
01:15:20,150 --> 01:15:20,583
-Oh, boy.

2416
01:15:20,617 --> 01:15:21,283
Oh, boy.

2417
01:15:21,317 --> 01:15:22,552
I think that's the coolest part.

2418
01:15:22,585 --> 01:15:24,453
-Well, the other side
was really cool too.

2419
01:15:24,487 --> 01:15:25,588
-Yeah, it sure was.

2420
01:15:25,622 --> 01:15:27,057
-Oh, wow.

2421
01:15:27,090 --> 01:15:29,258
Those little dangly things
at the top are the coolest.

2422
01:15:29,292 --> 01:15:30,060
-I don't know.

2423
01:15:30,093 --> 01:15:31,227
I don't know.

2424
01:15:31,260 --> 01:15:32,762
I think-- come on, look
at the top half there.

2425
01:15:32,795 --> 01:15:34,097
Top half near the sides.

2426
01:15:34,130 --> 01:15:35,532
That's the coolest part.

2427
01:15:35,565 --> 01:15:36,600
-I don't see it.

2428
01:15:36,633 --> 01:15:37,300
-Well, maybe.

2429
01:15:37,333 --> 01:15:38,233
I don't know.

2430
01:15:38,267 --> 01:15:40,603
Hey, wouldn't two of
them be even cooler?

2431
01:15:40,637 --> 01:15:41,237
-No way.

2432
01:15:41,270 --> 01:15:41,937
Nuh-uh.

2433
01:15:41,970 --> 01:15:44,040
-But 18 of them, whoa-ho, wow.

2434
01:15:44,073 --> 01:15:45,375
-That would be really cool.

2435
01:15:45,408 --> 01:15:46,142
-That would be great.

2436
01:15:46,175 --> 01:15:46,977
-Boy oh boy.

2437
01:15:47,010 --> 01:15:47,978
-Like you could-- yeah.

2438
01:15:48,012 --> 01:15:49,145
Hey, guys, look at that.

2439
01:15:49,178 --> 01:15:49,812
-What?

2440
01:15:49,845 --> 01:15:51,047
-Wait for it to come around.

2441
01:15:51,080 --> 01:15:51,882
OK.
Now!

2442
01:15:51,915 --> 01:15:52,448
-Wow!

2443
01:15:52,482 --> 01:15:54,250
-Oh man, that's so cool.

2444
01:15:54,283 --> 01:15:56,018
-That's real deece.

2445
01:15:56,052 --> 01:15:56,685
-Unbelievable.

2446
01:15:56,718 --> 01:15:59,322
Oh, now it knows it's cool.

2447
01:15:59,355 --> 01:16:00,423
It's not that cool anymore.
Let's get out of here.

2448
01:16:00,457 --> 01:16:01,590
-Yeah, I'm coming down now.

2449
01:16:01,623 --> 01:16:03,560
It was pretty cool while it
lasted though, wasn't it?

2450
01:16:03,593 --> 01:16:05,062
-I still think it's pretty cool.

2451
01:16:05,095 --> 01:16:06,062
You know.

2452
01:16:06,095 --> 01:16:07,930
It was not as cool as
before, but-- hey, guys.

2453
01:16:07,963 --> 01:16:09,032
Hey, hey, guys!

2454
01:16:09,065 --> 01:16:09,532
Look!

2455
01:16:09,565 --> 01:16:10,467
-What, what what?

2456
01:16:10,500 --> 01:16:10,967
-Look.

2457
01:16:11,000 --> 01:16:12,602
-Oh, wow.

2458
01:16:12,635 --> 01:16:13,235
Oh, cool.

2459
01:16:13,268 --> 01:16:14,270
-Baby, that's the coolest.

2460
01:16:14,303 --> 01:16:15,270
-Yeah.

2461
01:16:15,303 --> 01:16:17,473
-Hey, Joel, it's cool
and all, but what is it?

2462
01:16:17,507 --> 01:16:18,842
-Uh, it's--

2463
01:16:18,875 --> 01:16:21,878
-Well, at least
we know it's cool.

2464
01:16:21,912 --> 01:16:23,480
-Hey, do you know what it is?

2465
01:16:23,513 --> 01:16:25,948
-If you think you do, send
your description, drawing,

2466
01:16:25,982 --> 01:16:28,585
orthographic projection,
or expensive working model

2467
01:16:28,618 --> 01:16:33,090
to Cool Thing, care of MST 3000
Information Club, post office

2468
01:16:33,123 --> 01:16:38,195
box 5325, Hopkins
Minnesota, 55343.

2469
01:16:38,228 --> 01:16:39,428
-What do you think, sirs?

2470
01:16:39,462 --> 01:16:40,530
Pretty cool, huh?

2471
01:16:40,563 --> 01:16:41,832
-I don't think it's cool.

2472
01:16:41,865 --> 01:16:43,433
I think it's subterfuge.

2473
01:16:43,467 --> 01:16:45,602
I think you're just trying
to hide the fact that we're

2474
01:16:45,635 --> 01:16:47,370
winning and you're losing.

2475
01:16:47,403 --> 01:16:49,472
Put the movie back in, Frank.

2476
01:16:49,505 --> 01:16:52,808
-It's so simple, yet
so beautiful in a way.

2477
01:16:52,842 --> 01:16:57,047
There's a kind of arcane
beauty, you know. [WHEEZING]

2478
01:16:57,080 --> 01:16:58,915
-We need to talk.

2479
01:16:58,948 --> 01:17:00,550
Go away.

2480
01:17:00,583 --> 01:17:08,825
[MUSIC PLAYING]

2481
01:17:08,858 --> 01:17:12,795
-Meanwhile, in a less
interesting part of the film.

2482
01:17:12,828 --> 01:17:14,563
-Man was that ever
action-packed.

2483
01:17:14,597 --> 01:17:16,633
We better take a
15 minute break.

2484
01:17:20,570 --> 01:17:23,773
-All you scientists lose your
marbles every now and then.

2485
01:17:23,807 --> 01:17:25,408
-I never lose my marbles.

2486
01:17:25,442 --> 01:17:26,642
I'm a scientist.

2487
01:17:26,675 --> 01:17:29,512
-I'm sorry to have caused you
so much trouble, Lieutenant.

2488
01:17:29,545 --> 01:17:31,415
I woke up a little earlier
than the rest of this morning.

2489
01:17:31,448 --> 01:17:32,882
seemed a swell chance
to grab some pictures.

2490
01:17:32,915 --> 01:17:35,552
-I'm a morning person,
what can I say?

2491
01:17:35,585 --> 01:17:36,653
ROSTOV (OFFSCREEN):
Think I'd let

2492
01:17:36,687 --> 01:17:40,190
you get yourself killed
in a prehistoric jungle?

2493
01:17:40,223 --> 01:17:41,725
We've been friends too long.

2494
01:17:41,758 --> 01:17:43,827
-(GERMAN ACCENT) I
love you, damn it.

2495
01:17:43,860 --> 01:17:45,895
-Well, the, uh, the
leg's feeling better.

2496
01:17:45,928 --> 01:17:48,098
Come on, let's
keep walking, huh?

2497
01:17:48,132 --> 01:17:49,332
-My god, he's cheerful.

2498
01:17:49,365 --> 01:17:50,467
It's hard to get mad at him.

2499
01:17:50,500 --> 01:17:51,000
-Mm-hm.

2500
01:17:57,207 --> 01:17:58,608
-[HUMS] Thunk.

2501
01:17:58,642 --> 01:17:59,943
Oh, my head. [HUMS] Thunk.

2502
01:17:59,977 --> 01:18:02,645
Oh, my head. [HUMS] Thunk.

2503
01:18:02,678 --> 01:18:03,347
Oh, my head.

2504
01:18:06,115 --> 01:18:07,050
-Oh, check it out.

2505
01:18:07,083 --> 01:18:08,252
Graffiti from the Donner party.

2506
01:18:08,285 --> 01:18:08,785
Hm.

2507
01:18:11,822 --> 01:18:13,090
-(GERMAN ACCENT)
Candy cigarette?

2508
01:18:13,123 --> 01:18:14,623
They're toasted.

2509
01:18:14,657 --> 01:18:15,625
-Thanks.

2510
01:18:15,658 --> 01:18:16,858
-I think.

2511
01:18:16,892 --> 01:18:21,297
-You're not disturbed because
it's still BC instead of AD?

2512
01:18:21,330 --> 01:18:24,300
-No, because I'm
AC and you're DC.

2513
01:18:24,333 --> 01:18:25,302
-CD?

2514
01:18:25,335 --> 01:18:26,437
-C.

2515
01:18:26,470 --> 01:18:28,405
-ASAP.

2516
01:18:28,438 --> 01:18:29,405
-It's smoother.

2517
01:18:29,438 --> 01:18:30,807
I'm energized.

2518
01:18:30,840 --> 01:18:33,810
-I also said if I was
wrong, I'd apologize.

2519
01:18:33,843 --> 01:18:36,012
-That's not necessary.

2520
01:18:36,045 --> 01:18:37,613
-OK, enough of that, then.

2521
01:18:37,647 --> 01:18:40,650
-Don't ask me why,
but at the beginning,

2522
01:18:40,683 --> 01:18:43,120
I had you figured for
playing on the wrong team.

2523
01:18:43,153 --> 01:18:43,620
-Hm.

2524
01:18:43,653 --> 01:18:44,888
-I know.

2525
01:18:44,922 --> 01:18:46,055
I'm a Russian.

2526
01:18:46,088 --> 01:18:48,425
-I thought you were German.

2527
01:18:48,458 --> 01:18:51,528
-Almost my whole life has been a
witch hunt, one way or another.

2528
01:18:51,562 --> 01:18:53,230
-Did you ever get a
wedgie in grade school?

2529
01:18:53,263 --> 01:18:56,298
- -- Hitler and one of
his concentration camps.

2530
01:18:56,332 --> 01:18:57,567
-Hitler, what a jerk.

2531
01:18:57,600 --> 01:19:00,103
-And then my wife
and unborn child

2532
01:19:00,137 --> 01:19:04,875
died in another, with
Russian-made barbed wire.

2533
01:19:04,908 --> 01:19:07,343
It doesn't change them much.

2534
01:19:07,377 --> 01:19:10,947
-A lot of guys are finding
that out every day.

2535
01:19:10,980 --> 01:19:14,050
-All respect for my country
lies dead in those camps.

2536
01:19:14,083 --> 01:19:15,185
-I guess I should like you now.

2537
01:19:15,218 --> 01:19:17,487
-I hope to go back and
resurrect it some day.

2538
01:19:17,520 --> 01:19:18,255
-Mm-hm.

2539
01:19:18,288 --> 01:19:19,122
-Hm.

2540
01:19:19,155 --> 01:19:22,392
-No country can
survive when it loses

2541
01:19:22,425 --> 01:19:25,495
the respect of its own
people or of the world.

2542
01:19:25,528 --> 01:19:27,130
-Oh, it hasn't stopped
the old US of A.

2543
01:19:27,163 --> 01:19:30,733
-You go back, Mike, the
way you want to go back.

2544
01:19:30,767 --> 01:19:32,868
-My god, your hat smells good.

2545
01:19:32,902 --> 01:19:36,072
- --fighting for the
same thing, even if--

2546
01:19:36,105 --> 01:19:37,272
-Uh, line?

2547
01:19:37,305 --> 01:19:39,442
- --heave forbid, it means
pushing buttons on more rockets

2548
01:19:39,475 --> 01:19:40,743
like the one out
there somewhere.

2549
01:19:40,777 --> 01:19:41,745
-Blowing up the world.

2550
01:19:41,778 --> 01:19:45,115
-Heaven forbid, Joe.

2551
01:19:45,148 --> 01:19:47,050
-What did you call me?

2552
01:19:47,083 --> 01:19:47,717
-Let's go home.

2553
01:19:52,955 --> 01:19:54,123
-You take that Walkman off.

2554
01:19:54,157 --> 01:19:55,425
You're closing out the world.

2555
01:19:55,458 --> 01:19:57,960
- --coming up the mountain,
being forced through the earth

2556
01:19:57,993 --> 01:19:58,962
at tremendous pressures.

2557
01:19:58,995 --> 01:20:00,197
-(GERMAN ACCENT) I
know what that is.

2558
01:20:00,230 --> 01:20:02,532
-Too much pressure
with no safety valve.

2559
01:20:02,565 --> 01:20:03,733
-That's colitis, all right.

2560
01:20:03,767 --> 01:20:06,570
-If it should ever react
with the uranium fields--

2561
01:20:06,603 --> 01:20:08,003
-Like the fourth of July.

2562
01:20:08,037 --> 01:20:08,938
-Or the end of the world.

2563
01:20:12,542 --> 01:20:13,843
-Oh, that's it.

2564
01:20:13,877 --> 01:20:14,843
-That's what?

2565
01:20:14,877 --> 01:20:15,745
-A whole lot of batteries.

2566
01:20:15,778 --> 01:20:16,613
-It's the end of the road.

2567
01:20:16,647 --> 01:20:18,782
The Geiger can't
take us any further.

2568
01:20:18,815 --> 01:20:21,217
-He means that we are right
in the middle of the uranium

2569
01:20:21,250 --> 01:20:23,687
deposits and they
seem to explain why.

2570
01:20:23,720 --> 01:20:24,420
-I know what it means.

2571
01:20:24,453 --> 01:20:25,120
Shut up.

2572
01:20:25,153 --> 01:20:25,922
-Then how do we find the rocket?

2573
01:20:25,955 --> 01:20:27,757
-I wish I knew.

2574
01:20:27,790 --> 01:20:29,492
It could be anyplace.

2575
01:20:29,525 --> 01:20:31,928
-You mean we can wander
around up here for weeks?

2576
01:20:31,962 --> 01:20:32,828
Come on.

2577
01:20:32,862 --> 01:20:35,165
Let's get out of this graveyard.

2578
01:20:35,198 --> 01:20:36,065
-Yeah.

2579
01:20:36,098 --> 01:20:36,867
-Let's blow.

2580
01:20:36,900 --> 01:20:37,600
I hate this place.

2581
01:20:37,633 --> 01:20:38,935
-I agree, let's go.

2582
01:20:38,968 --> 01:20:41,203
-This stinks.

2583
01:20:41,237 --> 01:20:42,872
-Well, and here we are again.

2584
01:20:42,905 --> 01:20:46,275
Look, even Cesar's
sick of it by now.

2585
01:20:46,308 --> 01:20:48,678
-I don't know how long
these guys will hold out.

2586
01:20:48,712 --> 01:20:50,580
They've got pleats in
their pants, [INAUDIBLE].

2587
01:20:50,613 --> 01:20:51,982
-What's eating you?

2588
01:20:52,015 --> 01:20:53,883
-Making this movie is
probably the worst three days

2589
01:20:53,917 --> 01:20:57,653
of my life, that's what.

2590
01:20:57,687 --> 01:21:00,757
-We're beating our brains up
for something we may never find.

2591
01:21:00,790 --> 01:21:02,158
We don't even know
where to look now.

2592
01:21:02,192 --> 01:21:03,260
If the rocket's
up here, it'll rot

2593
01:21:03,293 --> 01:21:04,527
before anybody can get to it.

2594
01:21:04,560 --> 01:21:05,395
-You bored?

2595
01:21:05,428 --> 01:21:06,328
-Yes, I'm bored.

2596
01:21:06,362 --> 01:21:07,030
Let's do something.

2597
01:21:07,063 --> 01:21:08,898
-Yeah, I'm bored.

2598
01:21:08,932 --> 01:21:12,602
We're down to our last
few rounds of ammo.

2599
01:21:12,635 --> 01:21:13,837
Worried?

2600
01:21:13,870 --> 01:21:14,870
No.

2601
01:21:14,903 --> 01:21:15,372
-Scared?

2602
01:21:15,405 --> 01:21:16,607
A little.

2603
01:21:16,640 --> 01:21:17,107
-Aroused?

2604
01:21:17,140 --> 01:21:18,107
You bet.

2605
01:21:18,140 --> 01:21:19,542
-If I see more of those
fugitives from the zoo,

2606
01:21:19,575 --> 01:21:22,378
I'll just walk up to them
and kill them with spit.

2607
01:21:22,412 --> 01:21:23,513
-Oh, it just might work.

2608
01:21:23,547 --> 01:21:25,548
You haven't brushed
since we got here.

2609
01:21:25,582 --> 01:21:27,283
-Hey, this is fun.

2610
01:21:27,317 --> 01:21:27,817
I'm having fun.

2611
01:21:27,850 --> 01:21:28,618
How about you guys?

2612
01:21:28,652 --> 01:21:29,118
Yeah.

2613
01:21:29,152 --> 01:21:30,252
Neat.

2614
01:21:30,285 --> 01:21:32,588
-Are you speaking for
yourself, Danny, or for them.

2615
01:21:32,622 --> 01:21:34,490
-Maybe I'm saying what
they don't want to say.

2616
01:21:34,523 --> 01:21:35,525
-What are you saying?

2617
01:21:35,558 --> 01:21:36,793
I don't understand any of this.

2618
01:21:36,827 --> 01:21:37,893
-Lieutenant seems
to think you've

2619
01:21:37,927 --> 01:21:39,028
go something on your minds.

2620
01:21:39,062 --> 01:21:40,663
-Water on the brain.
[LAUGHS] Tease.

2621
01:21:40,697 --> 01:21:42,232
-I'll give you a chance
to unburden yourselves.

2622
